\par\par \b \uc2 *DJ Brazil Real Opens Weaker, Breaches BRL2.18/dlr Barrier\b0\par\par\uc2 312 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Juni 2013\par 14:29\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2013, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 18 Jun 2013 08:30 EDT *DJ  Traders See Possible \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil Central Bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Tuesday\par \par \uc2 18 Jun 2013 08:31 EDT *DJ  \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  Real Weaker Ahead of Wednesday Fed Meeting in U.S.\par \par \uc2 18 Jun 2013 08:45 EDT DJ  Brazil Real Opens Weaker, Breaches BRL2.18 Barrier Versus Dollar\par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO--The Brazilian real opened weaker against the U.S. dollar Tuesday, one day ahead of a closely watched meeting of the U.S. Federal Reserve's Open Market Committee that could offer signals about the direction of American monetary policy.\par \par \uc2 The real opened at BRL2.1811 to the dollar, weaker than the Monday close of BRL2.1734.\par \par \uc2 The real has been weakening steadily against the dollar in recent days, breaching what traders once thought was a firm barrier at BRL2.15 against the greenback.\par \par \uc2 Traders said renewed \uc2 Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market was possible Tuesday. The bank \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  after market hours Monday, selling the equivalent of nearly $2 billion in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  swap contracts at an auction. The contracts offer investors the opportunity to obtain bonds indexed directly to the U.S. dollar. Typically, the auctions help strengthen the real by increasing opportunities for investors to hedge in dollars.\par \par \uc2 But traders also said the real could continue weakening ahead of the crucial Fed meeting.\par \par \uc2 The Fed is widely expected to reduce, and eventually eliminate, its policy of freshening U.S. dollar supplies in global markets via the purchase of bonds. Such a policy would tend to raise U.S. interest rates and pull investments back to the U.S. Emerging market countries such as Brazil would likely find investment inflows dwindling in such a scenario.\par \par \uc2 Write to Tom Murphy at tom.murphy@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 June 18, 2013 08:45 ET (12:45 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020130618e96i002mk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
