\par\par \b \uc2 Brazil  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\uc2  sees 'no limits' for interest rate hikes -paper\b0\par\par\uc2 403 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Juni 2013\par 13:40\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Tombini hints Selic could go back to double-digit levels\par \par \uc2 * C.bank to keep \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets\par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO, June 17 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will not shy away from hiking interest rates back to double-digit levels if inflation fails to slow, president \uc2 Alexandre Tombini\uc2  said in an interview published on Monday by a local newspaper.\par \par \uc2 "Monetary policy will do what needs to be done," Tombini told Valor. When asked if it could raise the Selic rate back to double-digit levels, he replied: "We don't have a limit, nor a budget (for the rate hike cycle)."\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raised its benchmark Selic rate to 8.00 percent last month, the second increase in a row, after slashing it to 7.25 percent last year to boost the economy. Economists expect the bank to raise them to 9 percent in coming months, according to a weekly \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  survey.\par \par \uc2 Benchmark interest rates in Brazil have stayed above 10 percent for most years since the country's real \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was adopted in 1994.\par \par \uc2 Creating the conditions for keeping rates below that level was championed as one of President \uc2 Dilma Rousseff\uc2 's main achievements until high inflation started to eat into her record-high popularity rates and erode businesses' confidence.\par \par \uc2 Tombini also reiterated that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to reduce volatility, selling dollars in the spot market, offering swap contracts or auctioning off credit lines in dollars. The exchange rate will remain flexible, though, according to the interview.\par \par \uc2 "You never know if the exchange rate will go to 2.15 (per dollar), if it will improve, or if it will head towards 2.20."\par \par \uc2 Tombini also played down concerns about the impact of any measures by the U.S. Federal Reserve to scale back the massive stimulus offered in recent years to prop up its economy.\par \par \uc2 Fears of an imminent reversal of those policies have sent the real and other emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  lower in the past few weeks as investors anticipated capital outflows from developing countries.\par \par \uc2 "We know how to manage cashflows and we know how to manage money markets in times of stress. We were born in such environment," Tombini said.\par \par \uc2 BRAZIL-ECONOMY/TOMBINI | ABN | D | E | RBN | M | NAT | C | O | U | MTL | GRO | SOF | OIL | RNP | DNP | PTD   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020130617e96h000jt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
