\par\par \b \uc2 Brazil's Real Closes Sharply Weaker as  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Stays on Sidelines\b0\par\par\uc2 801 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Juni 2013\par 21:22\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 --\b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  after active trading closes\par \par \uc2 --Local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market "anxious" ahead of U.S. Federal Reserve meeting, trader says\par \par \uc2 --Brazilian real exits active trading at BRL2.1734 to the U.S. dollar\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par   By Jeff Fick \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 RIO DE JANEIRO--Brazil's real ended sharply weaker against the U.S. dollar Monday as the nation's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  opted to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market after the session had ended.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The real exited active trading at BRL2.1734 to the U.S. dollar, weaker from Friday's closing price fix of BRL2.1439, according to Tullett Prebon via FactSet. That was the real's weakest level since May 2009.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 However, Brazil's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  recovered slightly in after-hours trading after the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sold the equivalent of $1.96 billion in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  swap contracts. The real was at BRL2.1629 per dollar as of 2020 GMT. The swap contracts offer investors the opportunity to exchange bonds indexed to domestic interest rates for paper tied directly to the U.S. dollar. In essence, the swaps increase the market availability of financial instruments hedged in U.S. dollars. Typically, the swap auctions lead to a weakening of the U.S. dollar and a strengthening of the Brazilian real.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Central bankers had remained on the sidelines during Monday's session as the real weakened past the key BRL2.15 level. The decision to stand pat was criticized by traders, who noted that the bank had \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  last week when the real reached similar levels.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "This is really strange," said Marcos Trabbold, chief of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at Sao Paulo trading house BT Associados. "The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  shouldn't let the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reach these levels because it doesn't benefit anybody."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The weaker real only adds to Brazil's inflationary pressures, with consumer prices in May rising at the 6.5% ceiling of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's target range for inflation, Mr. Trabbold added.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 When the real breached BRL2.16 and quickly followed that move up with a jump past BRL2.17, the market showed that it was "demanding liquidity" that only the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  could provide, Mr. Trabbold said. But only after the session ended did the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  step in.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Traders were expected to test the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  during Monday's session amid expectations that the U.S. Federal Reserve, which is meeting this week, will give global markets a signal that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 monetary authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will start to taper its asset-buying program known as quantitative easing. The Fed will release its policy statement Wednesday, followed by comments from Chairman \f28 \uc2 \f2 Ben Bernanke\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The expected end to easy global monetary policy, which has allowed foreign investors flush with cheap cash to seek out higher rates of return in emerging markets such as Brazil, has caused the real in recent sessions to slide to its weakest level against the dollar in four years.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The market is very anxious about the results of the meeting," said Reginaldo Siaca, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  manager at Sao Paulo's Advanced brokerage. While trading fundamentals weren't behind the real's sharp move Monday, Mr. Siaca warned that the real could lose its "reference point" and decline to BRL2.20 per dollar if the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  fails to stem volatility.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Also overhanging the real was another downbeat outlook for the economy in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's weekly market survey, released before trading started Monday. Economists and market analysts once again cut their forecast for economic growth this year to 2.49%--well off the 3.5% expected at the start of 2013. Estimates for year-end inflation rose to 5.83%, causing the outlook for interest rates to climb to 9%. Brazil's benchmark Selic base interest rate currently stands at 8% after the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  implemented interest rate increases at its past two meetings.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In an interview with local newspaper Valor Economico, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  President \f28 \uc2 \f2 Alexandre Tombini\f28 \uc2 \f2  indicated that interest rates would rise as much as necessary to force inflation to converge on the bank's 4.5% target. "Monetary policy will do whatever needs to be done," Mr. Tombini said. In May, inflation was running at the 6.5% ceiling of the government's tolerance range.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Typically, indications that local interest rates were trending higher would be enough to support the real against the dollar, but that is not the case right now, market watchers said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "If the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  doesn't \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the real is going to continue to weaken until the market is balanced," Mr. Trabbold said. The head trader estimated that the real could trade between BRL2.18 and BRL2.20 later this week without \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  action.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Write to Jeff Fick at Jeff.Fick@dowjones.com [ 17-06-13 2033GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1156 1843   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020130617e96h000mb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
