\par\par \b \uc2 DJ  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  HORIZONS: This Ain't No Emerging Market Crisis\b0\par\par\uc2 715 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Juni 2013\par 08:29\par \uc2 Dow Jones Korean Newswires English Content\par \uc2 RTNWK\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2013, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 By Michael J. Casey\par \par \uc2 Don't confuse the recent rout in developing countries' \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  with an "emerging market crisis."\par \par \uc2 In a period of slowing global growth in which the Federal Reserve is seen reining in its monetary stimulus, most developing world governments are more than happy to see their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  weaken. In fact, it's part of a natural and necessary rebalancing in the world.\par \par \uc2 As U.S. bond yields rise and American growth prospects appear to outshine those of other places, money is being reallocated back to dollar assets. Meanwhile, with the Japanese yen still down 20% from its lows of September, emerging market producers are worried about lost competitiveness for their exporters. It's why many central banks are holding off on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and letting their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  fall. Softer exchange rates are just what the doctor ordered.\par \par \uc2 That is, unless you belong to a group I'll call the TIBS.\par \par \uc2 With apologies to \uc2 Jim O'Neill\uc2  of BRICS coinage fame and to whomever came up with that jarring PIGS acronym, TIBS stands for Turkey, India, Brazil and South Africa. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of these countries have borne the brunt of the emerging market selloff this month. That's because domestic political and economic problems make them more vulnerable to outflows of money--to the point that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  sell-off risks igniting national crises. The TIBS are the exceptions that prove the rule.\par \par \uc2 Turkey, India and \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  have all aggressively \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to bolster their flagging \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . They simply can't afford to take the sanguine view of depreciation that, say, Chile or Malaysia are taking.\par \par \uc2 In Brazil's case, it's an inflation problem. Brazil's failure to tackle rigidities in its labor market has left its inflation rate at the very top of its \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's acceptable range, which is why it hiked rates by an unexpectedly large half-percentage point two weeks ago even as economic growth was showing signs of grinding to a halt. The last thing the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  needs is a plunging Brazilian real, which would generate inflation via higher import prices.\par \par \uc2 Turkey and India, the other prominent \b \highlight22\uc2 interveners\b0 \highlight\uc2 , have successfully beaten back the inflation bogey that nagged them two years ago, but the legacy of that fight and the problem of large current-account deficits mean investors continue to fear a loss of market confidence in the Indian rupee or Turkish lira. (Complicating matters for Turkey is the political uncertainty sown by the violent protests against Prime Minister \uc2 Recep Tayyip Erdogan\uc2 .)\par \par \uc2 The TIBS member that's really in trouble, however, is South Africa. As with Brazil, South African inflation is stubbornly high -- just a hair below 6%, the top of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's target range. But unlike the other three countries, it doesn't have a deep pool of dollars with which to prop up the South African rand. Following a 4.3% plunge last month, the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves stood at a modest $48 billion at the end of May. It means that South Africa's authorities can only pray that the rout in the rand doesn't go so far as to fuel imported inflation. That would simply add to the country's long list of problems, which include industrial strife in its all-important mining sector and international declines in prices of the minerals its sells.\par \par \uc2 Yet one of the principal causes of South Africa's pain--a slowdown in China--provides a kind of mixed blessing to other emerging markets. Few are happy about weaker Chinese demand, of course, but at least it means they don't have to worry about inflation. Over the weekend, China reported that producer prices were down 2.9% on the year. For a country that manufactures so much of what we all buy, that's a powerful disinflationary force to unleash on the world. It means that for almost all countries outside the TIBS group, emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be free to keep falling if they have to.\par \par \uc2 Write to Michael Casey at michael.j.casey@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 June 11, 2013 14:28 ET (18:28 GMT)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 12-06-13 0629GMT\par \par \uc2 Dokument RTNWK00020130612e96c0000j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
