\par\par \b \uc2 Brazil's Real Opens Stronger as Markets Recover, Measures Awaited\b0\par\par\uc2 376 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Juni 2013\par 14:36\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO--Brazil's real strengthened for a second day on Wednesday as investors await new measures by the Brazilian government to encourage inflows and as global markets recovered from the previous day's volatility.\par \par \uc2 The real opened at 2.1244 per dollar, according to Tullett Prebon via FactSet, stronger than the previous day's close at BRL2.1355 to the dollar.\par \par \uc2 Many investors were expecting the government to announce Tuesday that they would ease taxes and other restrictions on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  inflows. Finance Minister Guido Mantega met with President \uc2 Dilma Rousseff\uc2  during the day, but didn't introduce any changes in policy. The last time the two met, Mr. Mantega said the government was reducing the so-called IOF tax on fixed-income investments held by foreigners -- a measure put in place originally to keep the real from strengthening too quickly.\par \par \uc2 According to analysts, the market is still expecting some easing of rules to encourage the entrance of foreign investors, especially as the Federal Reserve gives signals it will reverse stimulus measures. Those stimulus measures encouraged investment in emerging markets, and their possible end is expected to bring a massive slowdown in the flow of money to those markets.\par \par \uc2 After the massive sell-off of emerging markets on Tuesday, Brazil rebounded Wednesday along with a number of other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The Indian rupee, the South African rand, the Turkish lira and the Mexican peso also partially reversed losses on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 The repeated \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market by \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in recent days is also keeping the real relatively in line. After the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakened to the lowest level against the dollar in four years earlier this week, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  carried out a number of auctions of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swap contracts. The swap contracts have the effect of adding dollars to the market and strengthening the real.\par \par \uc2 After two months without such auctions, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  piled on in the last two days, holding two auctions a day to contain weakness and signaling to the market that it wouldn't allow too much weakness in the real.\par \par \uc2 Write to Paulo Winterstein at paulo.winterstein@dowjones.com [ 12-06-13 1236GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1156 1843   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020130612e96c000h0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
