\par\par \b \uc2 DJ Emerging Markets See Money Exit\b0\par\par\uc2 1,267 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Juni 2013\par 20:41\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires\par \uc2 DJDN\par 1\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2013, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    (FROM THE WALL STREET JOURNAL ASIA 6/12/13) \par    By Alex Frangos and Daniel Inman in Hong Kong and Patrick McGroarty in Johannesburg \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Money streamed out of emerging markets Tuesday, destabilizing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , sinking stocks and creating headaches for policy makers already worried about faltering growth.\f28 \par \par \uc2 In the latest signs of turmoil, highflying stock markets fell sharply in Asia, while \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in South Africa, India and Turkey reeled in the face of a surging U.S. dollar. Mexico's peso, which just a month ago was trading at its strongest level in almost two years, moved to its weakest level since late November.\par \par \uc2 Emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  rebounded somewhat in New York trading hours but remained broadly weaker on the day. The volatile trading reflected the difficulties investors face in trying to predict when developed-world banks might start to withdraw stimulus and how such moves will ripple across global markets.\par \par \uc2 Yields on \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  bonds have risen as investors gird for the possibility that the Federal Reserve could ratchet back the monthly bond purchases it has been making in a bid to stimulate growth. That, along with a sense that the U.S. economy is outperforming the rest of the world, is making U.S. assets and the greenback look more attractive than relatively risky emerging-market stocks and \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 An exodus from emerging markets threatens to hurt the financing and growth prospects of developing economies that have come to rely on large inflows of foreign capital in the wake of the global financial crisis.\par \par \uc2 Investors' nervousness was exacerbated Tuesday by a continuation of weak economic data from emerging economies. The Philippines said exports dropped 7% in April compared with the year before, reinforcing a global slowdown in trade. Unrest in Turkey, investor disappointment over a \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  decision to leave its bond-buying program unchanged and renewed concerns over Europe's efforts to repair its fragile financial system added to the mix.\par \par \uc2 Asian stock markets were among the first hit. Thailand's benchmark index fell 5%, while the Philippines' sank 4.6%, the worst day for both markets since late 2011. Indonesia's lost 3.5%.\par \par \uc2 "It feels like the party is ending," said Howard Wong, managing director at Doric Capital Corp. in Hong Kong.\par \par \uc2 Facing the loss of foreign capital, central banks in these emerging markets have scrambled to prop up their home \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 India's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  dove into \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets Tuesday to stop the rupee's slide at a record low of 58.98 to the dollar before it recovered. Pressured to attract capital to the country, a top Indian economic official promised a new round of measures to allow foreign investment in currently restricted parts of the economy.\par \par \uc2 Turkey's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Tuesday announced new measures to attract capital after spending much of the past four years trying to stop too much money from flooding into its economy. That helped to stem the lira's fall to near a multiyear low against the dollar as police moved in on protests in Istanbul. Turkey's capital measures echoed Brazil's move earlier this month to eliminate a 6% tax on foreigners' bond investments.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, \uc2 Poland's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said last week that it sold foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up the tumbling zloty.\par \par \uc2 But the capital exodus has continued apace, especially for economies heavily dependent on commodities, whose prices have been falling. Foreigners have been selling local mining stocks and countries' prospects for generating \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  through commodity exports appear weaker than before.\par \par \uc2 In platinum- and gold-rich South Africa, the rand fell nearly 2% to 10.38 to the dollar, marking a 23% plunge from the beginning of this year, before recovering some of that ground. Australia, seen as a proxy for emerging markets in Asia because of its liquid \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and exposure to Chinese and Indian demand for its raw materials, also saw its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  plummet to its weakest point against the U.S. dollar since 2010. The Brazilian real faced fresh losses Tuesday as foreign investors continued to pull up stakes from Latin America's biggest economy.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stepped up \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the face of the rapid \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  depreciation that began on May 28, with a series of five \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  swap auctions in two weeks. The auctions allow investors to exchange bonds linked to domestic interest rates for paper indexed directly to the U.S. dollar. The auctions tend to stall depreciation, but not for long.\par \par \uc2 Just two months ago, a wave of money was coursing around the globe, searching for yield. The massive quantitative-easing program the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  announced on April 4 coincided with a rate cut from the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and expectations that the Federal Reserve would continue its $85 billion in monthly bond purchases for the foreseeable future.\par \par \uc2 Several things have changed. Fed Chairman Ben Bernanke telegraphed last month that bond buying could slow later this year. The \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2 's liquidity boost has heightened turmoil in financial markets, with a spike in Japanese bond yields and a 20% selloff of Japanese stocks.\par \par \uc2 And there is mounting evidence that these economies just aren't growing as fast as people expected, while growth prospects in the U.S. are holding up, causing investors to favor U.S. assets over emerging markets.\par \par \uc2 Chinese data in recent days showed exports grew only 1% in May from the year earlier, the slowest pace in 10 months, weighed down by poor demand from ailing customers in Europe. Brazil and India are both suffering from slowdowns that have confounded policy makers and taken the shine off what had been among the most promising big economies in the world.\par \par \uc2 South Africa's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  warned last week that growth this year will likely fall short of its already tepid forecast for gross domestic product to expand 2.4%. Thailand's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor said on Monday that the bank would soon lower its forecast for 5.1% growth this year.\par \par \uc2 Dimming growth expectations have also prompted a number of interest-rate cuts to prop up economies in South Korea, India and Thailand, Turkey and Israel.\par \par \uc2 India's economy is among the hardest hit by the recent reversal in capital flows. The country's slow growth-- less than 5% annually in the past two quarters compared with nearly 10% a few years ago-- and the weak rupee have made a fragile economy more vulnerable.\par \par \uc2 A weaker rupee makes it more expensive to pay for critical imports such as oil and coal. It also widens the government's already-large deficit, much of which goes to pay for fuel subsidies. More expensive imports also exacerbate inflation, limiting the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's ability to cut interest rates to spur growth.\par \par \uc2 South Africa is battling the same contradictory forces. As growth slows, the South African \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been reluctant to cut interest rates because annual inflation is threatening to breach its target ceiling of 6%.\par \par \uc2 The outflows had been mostly orderly and some \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakening was welcomed because over the long term it can make a country's exports more competitive globally. But the orderly pace changed Tuesday, when investors rushed for the exits.\par \par \uc2 "Until very recently the conversation was all about capital inflows causing asset-price bubbles," said Gareth Leather, an economist with Capital Economics in London. "It's switched very quickly."\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Khushita Vasant in Mumbai and Tom Murphy in Sao Paulo, Brazil, contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 June 11, 2013 14:41 ET (18:41 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020130611e96b004cv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
