\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Brazil \highlight\uc2  Finance Minister Mantega Sees Less  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  in  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Market - Report\b0\par\par\uc2 By Rogerio Jelmayer   \par \uc2 542 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Juni 2013\par 13:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO--\b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's government will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  less in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, the country's finance minister said, even though the Brazilian real is facing pressure to depreciate amid a rising deficit in its overseas accounts.\par \par \uc2 "Despite the volatility, we now have a \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  that's more flexible, more floating than at other times, with less \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Guido Mantega said in an interview published Thursday in the financial newspaper Valor Economico.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, the government stripped away a 6% tax called IOF on foreign purchases of local debt. The tax was originally put in place in 2010 to stabilize the nation's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the Brazilian real. Heavy financial inflows at that time were causing an unwanted \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation and harming exports of the world's second-largest developing economy after China.\par \par \uc2 "We are gradually retiring the IOF tax. We had retired it in the past from investments in equities and now we retired it for investments in fixed income," said the minister, who highlighted that country's \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy don't changed. "This is the same policy, adapted to the moment."\par \par \uc2 According to analysts, the move came as the deficit in the country's overseas transactions account is growing, and the government needs to attract dollars inflow.\par \par \uc2 Brazil's current account deficit, the sum of most non-investment capital inflows and outflows, widened to $70 billion in April, hitting the equivalent of 3% of gross domestic product for the first time since 2002. Last year, the deficit was $54 billion.\par \par \uc2 In past years, the current account deficit was offset by foreign direct investment, the kind of nuts-and-bolts factory expansion undertaken by foreign companies, as Brazil emerged as a source of growth while developed economies stagnated. But foreign direct investment, at $65 billion over the past 12 months, is no longer enough to close the gap.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Mantega said that in general the government is prompting less \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not acquiring dollars [on the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market]."\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had largely remained on the sidelines in recent weeks, despite the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  approaching the BRL2.15 mark. But a sharp sell-off in recent sessions forced the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 -swap contracts, which typically cause the real to strengthen against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 Still, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  hasn't been accumulating \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves. Total reserves so far this year fell around $4 billion to the current level of $374.9 billion.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Mantega said the measure isn't supposed to help with inflation, as that's the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's task, to be managed through the use of the key lending rate, the Selic.\par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  acted to contain accelerating inflation expectations and also to avoid a spread of inflation. It will not impact investments. This [Selic rate decisions] is a task of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate committee, not a task of President \uc2 Dilma Rousseff\uc2  nor mine," said Mr. Mantega.\par \par \uc2 Last week, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  surprised analysts increasing the Selic rate more than expected by 50 basis points to 8%.\par \par \uc2 Write to Rogerio Jelmayer at rogerio.jelmayer@dowjones.com [ 06-06-13 1100GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020130606e966000dv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
