\par\par \b \uc2 Brazil Takes Steps to Lure Back Foreign Capital\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jeff Fick   \par \uc2 785 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Juni 2013\par 22:15\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 After spending years discouraging return-seeking investors and their "hot money," Brazil is taking steps to lure foreign investment funds back.\par \par \uc2 But so far many investors aren't biting, underscoring the challenges facing the sixth-largest economy and policymakers struggling to rein in inflation in spite of subpar economic growth.\par \par \uc2 A day after Brazil's government abruptly scrapped a tax on bonds purchased with money coming in from outside the country, Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  neared a fresh four-year low. The tax was first implemented in October 2010 to fight off the billions of dollars of cash that was flooding Brazil's financial system and causing the real to strengthen, a situation that eroded the competitiveness of the country's exporters.\par \par \uc2 Abolishing the tax initially lifted the real when trading opened Wednesday morning. But the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  soon fell so far that \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support it. The real ended at 2.1325 per dollar, slightly stronger on the day.\par \par \uc2 The real's volatile performance on Wednesday was the latest sign that government and \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials are finding their usual tools are proving ineffective at stabilizing financial markets. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has dropped 4% since the start of last week, even as \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  hiked interest rates and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  twice in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, Brazilian Finance Minister Guido Mantega called lifting the tax a "long-term measure" that wasn't aimed at boosting the real.\par \par \uc2 Brazil's government has slapped taxes on a growing list of financial transactions since October 2010, ranging from overseas credit card purchases to foreign loans. In each case, the intention was to avoid an unwanted appreciation of the real by slowing the inflow of foreign capital.\par \par \uc2 Lately, Brazil's had the opposite problem. Investors have pulled $916 million this year through May 29 from funds that mainly buy Brazilian bonds, compared with nearly $20 million in outflows during the same period last year. Those outflows could accelerate if the Federal Reserve tightens monetary policy later this year, analysts say.\par \par \uc2 Some investors say the removal of the tax may actually encourage further selling of Brazilian bonds in the short term. Worries have been growing that Brazil's high yields wouldn't be enough to compensate for a swiftly declining \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , worsening trade flows and stubbornly high inflation. Yet until now, some investors were reluctant to sell, as they would have had to pay a 6% surcharge to get back in should conditions improve.\par \par \uc2 Lifting the tax "is not going to be that big a game changer going forward," said Naveen Kunam, senior portfolio manager at ING Investment Management, which manages $179 billion in assets.. Mr. Kunam's firm owns Brazilian bonds denominated in reais, but has started to reduce its exposure.\par \par \uc2 Brazil's economy expanded by only 0.9% in 2012, and economists forecast growth of 2.5% this year. At the same time, Brazil is also suffering a renewed bout of inflation. The country's inflation rate is currently 6.5%, the top end of the range the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has said it will tolerate.\par \par \uc2 For much of the last year, Brazilian policymakers have been torn over whether to make boosting growth or lowering inflation a priority. Last week, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  hiked its Selic base interest rate a half percentage point to 8.0%, saying in a statement it will pursue its strategy of tightening monetary policy to keep a lid on rising prices. However, higher interest rates may make it even more difficult for the economy to grow.\par \par \uc2 Reflecting Brazil's dim prospects, Brazil's benchmark Ibovespa stocks index is down 13% this year, compared with a 16% increase in the \uc2 Dow Jones Industrial\uc2  Average.\par \par \uc2 The government is betting that investors "will risk Brazil for better returns," setting aside worries about the country's sluggish economy to take advantage of higher yields than similar investments in emerging markets like Chile, Mexico and Turkey, said Durval Correa, a trader at Sao Paulo's MultiMoney brokerage.\par \par \uc2 The elimination of the tax could help lure investors back to Brazil if it makes progress on its other problems, said Michael Lee, a portfolio manager with Wells Fargo Advantage International Bond Fund, which manages $1.68 billion.\par \par \uc2 "Brazil still needs to do certain things to get their economy right, but compared to where yields are around the world, Brazil still looks attractive," Mr. Lee said. He's buying more real-denominated bonds now that the tax is lifted.\par \par \uc2 -\uc2 Tom Murphy\uc2 , Erin McCarthy, Prabha Natarajan and Rogerio Jelmayer contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Jeff Fick at jeff.fick@dowjones.com [ 05-06-13 2015GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020130605e965000oy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
