\par\par \b \uc2 DJ ICE Coffee, Sugar Move With Brazil  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Gyrations\b0\par\par\uc2 306 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Juni 2013\par 21:06\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2013, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK--Arabica-coffee and raw-sugar futures whipsawed Wednesday, mirroring the moves of the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Brazil, the world's biggest grower of both commodities.\par \par \uc2 Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strengthened in early trade after the government said it would scrap a special tax on foreign investments in bonds. Coffee and sugar prices gained as investors speculated that Brazilian producers and exporters would sit on the markets' sidelines.\par \par \uc2 The coffee and sugar markets are closely intertwined with the value of Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Growers sell their products abroad in U.S. dollars, so a weaker real is an incentive to ramp up exports because they receive more reais back.\par \par \uc2 "It hurts their bottom line" if the real is stronger, said Chris Narayanan, head of agricultural research at \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2 .\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  later \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, weakening the real.\par \par \uc2 Arabica coffee for delivery in July on ICE Futures U.S. ended 0.2% lower at $1.2745 a pound, after reaching $1.2950 a pound. Raw sugar for July delivery ended flat at 16.38 cents a pound after trading as high as 16.53 cents a pound and as low as 16.34 cents a pound.\par \par \uc2 Cotton for July delivery on ICE ended 1.2% lower at 83.52 cents a pound as traders skimmed profits after the market surged during the previous two sessions on concerns about near-term availability of the fiber.\par \par \uc2 Cocoa rose 1.1% to a two-week high of $2,308 a ton, while orange juice shed 1.7% to settle at $1.5050 a pound.\par \par \uc2 Write to Leslie Josephs at leslie.josephs@dowjones.com and Alexandra Wexler at alexandra.wexler@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 June 05, 2013 15:06 ET (19:06 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020130605e965004sk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
