\par\par \b \uc2 *DJ Brazil Real Weakens to Lowest Level in Four Years\b0\par\par\uc2 729 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Mai 2013\par 21:20\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2013, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 31 May 2013 15:22 EDT *DJ  Brazil Real Ends Active Trading At BRL2.1480/dlr\par \par \uc2 31 May 2013 15:22 EDT *DJ  Brazil Real Ends Active Trading At BRL2.1480/dlr\par \par \uc2 31 May 2013 15:23 EDT *DJ  \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  Real Weakens Despite \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank Intervention\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 31 May 2013 15:23 EDT *DJ  \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  Real Weaker On Worries About Current Account, U.S. Fed\par \par \uc2 31 May 2013 16:02 EDT DJ  Brazil Real Weakens to Lowest Level in Four Years\par \par \uc2 --Brazil real exits active trading at BRL2.1480/dollar\par \par \uc2 --Real at four-year low despite \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action\par \par \uc2 --Investors worry about widening current account deficit\par \par \uc2 By Tom Murphy\par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO--The Brazilian real ended active trading Friday at a four-year low, with more losses possible next week as investors re-evaluate the country's economic and investment prospects.\par \par \uc2 The real exited active trading at BRL2.1480 to the dollar, considerably weaker than the Wednesday close of BRL2.1084. Thursday was a religious holiday with Brazilian financial markets closed.\par \par \uc2 The Friday value for the real was the lowest since May 2009, a period when Brazil was still suffering direct effects of the global financial crisis.\par \par \uc2 The real piled up losses Friday despite \uc2 Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . In early afternoon trading, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  held a spot auction of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  swap contracts, selling $876.7 million worth of the contracts.\par \par \uc2 Typically, the sale of swap contracts helps strengthen the real against the dollar by making more dollar-hedged bonds available to investors. In fact, the auction initially helped strengthen the real slightly against the dollar. However, in later trading, investors once again pushed up the value of the greenback against the Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "This morning, there were those who said the real could depreciate to BRL2.20 against the dollar by the end of the year," said Mauriciano Cavalcanti, a trader at Sao Paulo's Ouro Minas brokerage. "Now, I would say we're going to reach BRL2.20 a lot sooner than that."\par \par \uc2 There are many reasons for the real's weakness.\par \par \uc2 "One reason is the fact that the U.S. dollar has been gaining against \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  world-wide," said Joao Paulo Correa, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at the southern Brazilian brokerage of Correparti. "The Brazilian real is no exception to that trend."\par \par \uc2 Although economists differ on the timing, few disagree with the idea that the U.S. Federal Reserve will have to reverse its long-standing policy of monetary loosening. Once that happens, investors may end up flocking to U.S. investments, all but abandoning emerging markets.\par \par \uc2 That process may have already begun, according to Mr. Cavalcanti. "Next week, we may get a better idea of how that is affecting the Brazilian market," he said.\par \par \uc2 Because of the intense focus on the Fed, investors in Brazil effectively shrugged off Brazil's own interest rate hike, which was ordered on Wednesday night. The \uc2 Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raised its base rate by a half point to 8.0% as part of a strategy against inflation.\par \par \uc2 The Brazilian rate hike had no effect on Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading Friday. However, it did hit hard at Brazilian stocks. In late afternoon trading, the benchmark Ibovespa index was down more than 2.5%, given that higher domestic interest rates will likely cause investors to shift from stocks to fixed-income investments.\par \par \uc2 Inflation is another of the factors making investors take another, and more skeptical, look at Brazil.\par \par \uc2 Brazil's inflation rate is currently running at a worrisome 6.5%. But economic growth in the first quarter was unexpectedly low, with the economy expanding only 0.6% against the fourth quarter of 2012, not the more robust 0.9% analysts had expected.\par \par \uc2 "This is the new strategy," said Paulo Faria-Tavares, managing partner of Sao Paulo's PTX Lending consultants. "Low growth and high interest rates."\par \par \uc2 Other problems include Brazil's fast-growing current account deficit. The deficit widened in April to a 12-month level of $70 billion, significantly greater than the year-end 2012 level of $54 billion. Simply put, that means more dollars going out than coming in. And the pace of the net outflow is increasing fast.\par \par \uc2 Write to Tom Murphy at tom.murphy@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 May 31, 2013 16:02 ET (20:02 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020130531e95v005p6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
