\par\par \b \uc2 Brazil's Real Closes Weaker for Fourth Straight Week\b0\par\par\uc2 By Paul Kiernan   \par \uc2 491 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Mai 2013\par 22:32\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 RIO DE JANEIRO--Brazil's real closed weaker against the dollar Friday to cap off a fourth straight week of gradual losses, as investors continued to question the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's ability to hold up amid deteriorating trade flows and the prospect of tighter monetary policies abroad.\par \par \uc2 The real exited active trading at BRL2.0482 per dollar, according to Tullett Prebon via FactSet, compared with BRL2.0430 at the end of Thursday's session.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has lost 2.4% of its value since April 26 and occasionally broke above the psychological threshold of BRL2.05 to the dollar in intraday trading. \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  at such levels in the past, remained on the sidelines.\par \par \uc2 Concerns weighing on the real are twofold. Recent talk of the U.S. Federal Reserve ending its bond-buying program known as quantitative easing, implemented during the 2008-09 crisis, could limit the amount of cash available to foreign investors attracted to Brazil's comparatively high interest rates.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, a persistent trade deficit so far this year has left some wondering if one of Brazil's key economic motors--exports of commodities such as iron ore and soybeans--is losing steam next to a growing appetite for imported goods.\par \par \uc2 Imports this year, through the third week of May, rose 7.3% from the same period in 2012 to $89.27 billion. Exports fell 5.9% to $84.18 billion.\par \par \uc2 Behind the rising imports and declining exports is an overvalued \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 : many goods and services cost much more in Brazil than abroad. A \uc2 McDonald's\uc2  quarter-pounder hamburger costs $5.37 in Rio de Janeiro, compared with $3.89 in Lower Manhattan.\par \par \uc2 Joao Querino de Santos, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader at Sao Paulo's J. Alves brokerage, said that if the real continues its slide, Brazilian importers with debts abroad could start to get nervous, potentially creating a scramble for dollars that could throw the market off balance.\par \par \uc2 "I think the importers are getting a little out of control," Mr. Santos said. "If the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  gets to BRL2.06 or BRL2.07, they'll definitely be buying dollars."\par \par \uc2 Mr. Santos said he thinks the real could weaken to BRL2.11 in June. But with a holiday-shortened week in Brazil coming up, things should be quiet, he added.\par \par \uc2 Next week nevertheless promises to be heavy on news and data, with the government due to report Brazil's first-quarter gross domestic product and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's monetary-policy committee scheduled to make a decision on interest rates Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 A majority of 12 economists and analysts surveyed by Dow Jones Newswires this week expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to increase its benchmark Selic rate by 0.50 percentage point, to 8%. A minority expect a quarter-point hike.\par \par \uc2 Write to Paul Kiernan at paul.kiernan@dowjones.com [ 24-05-13 2032GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1156 1843   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020130524e95o000mx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
