\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Latin American  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2  drop as Fed stimulus eyed\b0\par\par\uc2 454 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Mai 2013\par 17:56\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Fed governor says QE3 may end later this year\par \par \uc2 * Mexico growth slump suggests additional rate cuts\par \par \uc2 * Mexico peso drops 0.3 percent, Brazil real falls 0.5 pct\par \par \uc2 By Walter Brandimarte\par \par \uc2 RIO DE JANEIRO, May 16 (Reuters) - Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakened on Friday amid fears the U.S. Federal Reserve could end its bond-buying program by year-end, removing a key stimulus measure that has been fueling appetite for higher-yielding assets in emerging markets.\par \par \uc2 Further pressuring the Mexican peso was data showing the annual pace of growth in the first quarter was the weakest in three years. The numbers increased bets that Mexico may further cut its base interest rate later this year, reducing the allure of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The peso dropped 0.3 percent to an over three-week low of 12.3087 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 The Brazilian real slid 0.5 percent to a near four-month low of 2.0377 per greenback after San Francisco Fed President \uc2 John Williams\uc2  said the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could start to reduce its monetary stimulus this summer and end its bond purchases by the end of the year.\par \par \uc2 "That talk of more limited Fed stimulus is strengthening the dollar. Other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have been taking a beating from the dollar the whole week," said a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader at a large Brazilian bank.\par \par \uc2 So far this week, the real has lost some 0.9 percent against the U.S. dollar, nearing a level that could trigger a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , according to analysts.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  last acted in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market at the end of March, when the real neared the 2.03-per-dollar level.\par \par \uc2 Analysts believe that despite this year's poor performance in the country's trade balance, policymakers will not allow the real to weaken much further.\par \par \uc2 In the first four months of the year, Brazil has accumulated a trade deficit of $6.15 billion, a sharp contrast from the $3.299 billion surplus it recorded in the same period last year, according to government data released on May 2.\par \par \uc2 Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  prices at 1530 GMT:\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2                             Daily  YTD pct\par                                       pct   change\par                           Latest   change  \par Brazil real                 2.0377    -0.53     0.11\par                                            \par Mexico peso                12.3090    -0.29     4.51\par                                            \par Chile peso                480.8000    -0.17    -0.44\par                                            \par Colombia peso            1841.1000    -0.05    -4.08\par                                            \par Peru sol                    2.6450    -0.34    -3.55\par                                            \par Argentina peso              5.2325     0.00    -6.12\par (interbank)                                  \par                                            \par Argentina peso              8.9200     0.34   -23.99\par (parallel)                                   \par                                            \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Additional reporting by Natalia Cacioli in Sao Paulo, editing 