\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-US data weighs on Mexico peso, buoys other Latam  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 574 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Mai 2013\par 18:01\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Data raises concern about US economic recovery\par \par \uc2 * Data also suggests Fed will keep monetary stimulus\par \par \uc2 * Mexico peso drops 0.2 percent, Brazil real flat\par \par \uc2 By Walter Brandimarte\par \par \uc2 RIO DE JANEIRO, May 16 (Reuters) - Mexico's peso dropped on Thursday after surprisingly weak U.S. housing and jobs data weighed on prospects for the Mexican economy.\par \par \uc2 Other Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  had a slightly better performance, however, as the same data fueled hopes that U.S. monetary stimulus will remain in place.\par \par \uc2 The peso was 0.2 percent weaker at 12.23 per dollar, after hitting a three-week intraday low of 12.277. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was hurt by data that raised concerns about the economic recovery of the United States, Mexico's main trading partner.\par \par \uc2 The reports showed the number of Americans filing new claims for unemployment benefits climbed last week at the fastest pace in six months, while ground-breaking at home construction sites tumbled and consumer prices fell the most in over four years.\par \par \uc2 However, the same data eased fears that the U.S. Federal Reserve would scale back its bond-buying program, a decision that would remove a key source of global liquidity that has sustained investors' appetite for high-yielding emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "The dollar had been gaining in the past few days on a possible reduction in U.S. monetary stimulus. But the price data released today hinders any monetary tightening as there are no inflation pressures at the moment," said Martin Ferrer, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  desk manager at Capital \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Santiago.\par \par \uc2 The Brazilian real was practically unchanged at 2.0235 per dollar. Its losses were also capped by bets that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would prevent any \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  depreciation that could add to inflation, which is running just a tad below the ceiling of a government target.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market at the end of March, when the real neared the level of 2.03 per dollar. Analysts believe that, despite a poor performance in the country's trade balance, policymakers will not allow the real to weaken much further than that.\par \par \uc2 "While a weaker real could be a natural buffer for the widening current account deficit, the fiscal thrust and the tight labor market suggest that \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  depreciation could add more pressure to the IPCA," JPMmorgan's analysts wrote in a research note, referring to the country's key consumer inflation index, which is running at 6.49 percent in the 12-month period through April.\par \par \uc2 "The chatter from the policy circles points toward the need for a weaker real, but in real terms. A nominal depreciation accompanied by higher inflation would backfire just as it did in 2012," \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  added, revising its forecast for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to 2.05 from 1.95 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Latin American \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  prices at 1510 GMT:    \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2                          daily %    YTD %\par                             