\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Brazil, Mexico  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2  drop on economy concerns\b0\par\par\uc2 426 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Mai 2013\par 19:57\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Business activity data from China, Europe spur worries\par \par \uc2 * Brazil's balance of payment performance in focus\par \par \uc2 * Mexican peso, Brazilian real drop about 0.2 pct each\par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO, May 6 (Reuters) - The \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Brazil and Mexico edged lower on Monday as weak economic data in China and Europe set off a new round of caution over the global economic outlook, tempering the optimism on Friday after the U.S. jobs numbers.\par \par \uc2 Trading volumes were below average as Japanese and UK financial markets were closed for holidays.\par \par \uc2 The Mexican peso dropped 0.25 percent after hitting a three-week high on Friday as reports showed Chinese business growth in April hit its slowest pace since August 2011 and that recession continued to drag on euro zone companies, including in Germany.\par \par \uc2 The Brazilian real lost 0.2 percent against the U.S. dollar. Investors were becoming worried about a deterioration in Brazil's balance of payments, but were unwilling to make strong bets against the real on fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "Investors are not sure about the real situation of the Brazilian economy, so they keep trading cautiously," said Reginaldo Gualhardo, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  desk manager at Treviso brokerage in Sao Paulo.\par \par \uc2 Economic data last week showed Brazil's trade deficit deepened to nearly $1 billion in April, the latest sign of deterioration in the country's balance of payments. In March, Brazil posted its worst current account deficit on record for that month.\par \par \uc2 Despite the poor data, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has remained steady as \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has systematically \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep the real around the 2-per-dollar mark, a level that policymakers consider adequate to support exporters without adding to inflation pressure.\par \par \uc2 "Big news will be needed for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to move away from current levels," said Jose Carlos Amado, a trader with Renascenca brokerage in Sao Paulo.\par \par \uc2 Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  prices at 1730 GMT:\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2                            Daily  YTD pct\par                                      pct   change\par                          Latest   change  \par Brazil real                2.0130    -0.22     1.34\par                                           \par Mexico peso               12.1004    -0.25     6.31\par                                           \par Chile peso               469.3000     0.09     2.00\par                                           \par Colombia peso           1828.6000     0.31    -3.42\par                                           \par Peru sol                   2.6160     0.42    -2.48\par                                           \par Argentina peso             5.2050    -0.10    -5.62\par (interbank)                                 \par                                           \par Argentina peso             9.8300     0.51   -31.03\par (parallel)                                  \par                                           \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Reporting by Camila Moreira; writing by Walter Brandimarte; Editing by Leslie Adler)\f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-LATAM/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  | ABN | D | M | RBN | NAT | C | E | O | U | MTL | GRO | SOF | OIL | R