\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Latam  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2  gain on US jobs data; real edges lower\b0\par\par\uc2 387 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Mai 2013\par 17:07\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * US creates more jobs than expected in April\par \par \uc2 * Brazil industrial production disappoints in March\par \par \uc2 * Mexico peso up 0.7 per cent, Brazil real dips 0.1 per cent\par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO, May 3 (Reuters) - Most Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  strengthened on Friday after a stronger-than-expected U.S. jobs report eased concerns about a sharp slowdown in the world's largest economy, encouraging investors to take on risk in emerging markets.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Mexico and Chile gained 0.7 percent and 0.5 percent, respectively, after the report showed U.S. employers added 165,000 new jobs last month, more than the 145,000 expected by economists. The U.S. jobless rate fell to a four-year low of 7.5 percent as a result.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. data was especially relevant for Mexico, which sends most of its exports to its northern neighbor.\par \par \uc2 The Brazilian real softened slightly in thin trading, however, as investors had little incentive to bet on a stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  due to a weak domestic data and possible \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market.\par \par \uc2 Among the latest data underscoring \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's economic challenges, industrial production for March rose about half as much as investors expected. On Thursday, another report showed Brazil recorded in April its third traded deficit in four months as local industry struggled.\par \par \uc2 "The market is much quieter and investors are cautious due to domestic and external uncertainties," said Reginaldo Siaca, a manager at Advanced brokerage in Sao Paulo.\par \par \uc2 He noted the government seems to be comfortable with an exchange rate around 2 reais per dollar, which limits investors' willingness to make strong bets on different \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels.\par \par \uc2 Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  prices at 1450 GMT:\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2                          daily %    YTD %\par                                   change   change\par                          Latest           \par Brazil real                2.0117    -0.11     1.41\par                                           \par Mexico peso               12.0920     0.70     6.39\par                                           \par Chile peso               469.6000     0.45     1.94\par                                           \par Colombia peso           1837.8400     0.04    -3.91\par                                           \par Peru sol                   2.6280     0.76    -2.93\par                                           \par Argentina peso             5.1950    -0.05    -5.44\par (interbank)                                 \par                                           \par Argentina peso             9.8500    -2.23   -31.17\par (parallel)                                  \par                                           \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Reporting by Natalia Cacioli; Writing by Walter Brandimarte)\f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-LATAM/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  | ABN | D | M | RBN | NAT | C | E | O | U | MTL | GRO | SOF | OIL | RNP | DNP | PTD   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020130506e953000oz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
