\par\par \b \uc2 Brazil's Real Closes Weaker After Interest-Rate Hike\b0\par\par\uc2 546 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 April 2013\par 21:49\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 --Small Selic Increase Driving Bets Against the BRL\par \par \uc2 --Traders Believe \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Will Have to Help the Real\par \par \uc2 --\b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Market \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Driving Investors Away, Says Analyst\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Paulo Trevisani \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BRASILIA--The Brazilian real weakened against the dollar Thursday, in the first trading day after \f28 \uc2 \f2 Brazil's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  announced its first interest-rate increase in almost two years.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The BRL exited active trading at BRL2.0150 per dollar, roughly 0.8% down from Wednesday's closing of BRL1.9976, according to FactSet.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Late Wednesday, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  had changed to 7.5% the target for its benchmark rate, known as Selic. It was up from the 7.25% level the Selic had been since October.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The move also marked the reversal of a monetary easing cycle that began in August 2011, when the Selic was cut to 12% from 12.5%, and was subsequently trimmed every two months or so.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But inflation started to pick up in the past few months and government officials recently said inflation would not be tolerated. That drove many traders to bet the Selic would go to 7.75%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Future markets were [already] pricing in a tightening cycle," said Robert Wood, a Latin America specialist at the Economist Intelligence Unit in New York, but it came at "only 25" basic points, he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 An interest-rate increase usually drives up the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  value, but the relatively small rise seemed to have caused the opposite effect, with some players apparently believing inflation will remain strong and thus weaken the real.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "They will have to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to avoid further depreciation" of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , said Claudio Irigoyen, head of Latin America \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategy at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of America\f28 \uc2 \f2 Merrill Lynch\f28 \uc2 \f2  in New York. He thinks the BCB will act before the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  touches BRL2.05 per dollar. "Players understand that, so they go short on the dollar, long on BRL/dollar. They know the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will be on their side."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The lower-than-expected increase also means the real will have less of a carry than previously thought. That could make the BRL less attractive to yield-seeking investors.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But there could be other factors at play, as well, including a perception that Brazil has become less friendly to investors' hot money.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 For instance, some observers had expected the real to get stronger after the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan\f28 \uc2 \f2 's April 5 announcement of a massive bond-buying program, which would lower yields in Japan, thus driving investors to other markets, Brazil included. But Brazil hasn't seen much of that money so far, and some believe it is so because of capital controls in the South American nation.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The Brazilian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is closely managed," said Nick Cowley, of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Henderson Global Investors\f28 \uc2 \f2 , in London. He said the real will remain within the BRL1.95-to-BRL2.05 band it has traded in the past couple of months. However, the measures Brazilian government uses to keep that band could drive investors away.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "A lot of Japanese investors went to the Mexican peso" instead of the real because they see less government \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the Mexican market, Mr. Cowley said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Write to Paulo Trevisani at paulo.trevisani@dowjones.com [ 18-04-13 1949GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1156 1843   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020130418e94i000to\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
