\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Brazil real up on  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\uc2 , rates fall on Rousseff\b0\par\par\uc2 605 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2013\par 23:15\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil central bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  losses\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate futures drop on Rousseff's inflation comments\par \par \uc2 * Brazil real gains 0.3 pct, Mexico peso up 0.1 pct\par \par \uc2 By Walter Brandimarte\par \par \uc2 RIO DE JANEIRO, March 27 (Reuters) - The Brazilian real rose on Wednesday after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to stem \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  losses, while interest-rate futures dropped following comments by President Dilma Rousseff on inflation.\par \par \uc2 Other Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  started the day with losses as investors remained worried that the solution to Cyprus' banking crisis, which included heavy losses for large depositors, could be used as a template to resolve banking problems in other troubled euro-zone economies.\par \par \uc2 Later in the day, however, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Mexico and Colombia rebounded in thin trading as many traders took off for the Easter holidays. The Mexican peso rose 0.1 percent to 12.3450 per dollar while the Colombian peso gained 0.3 percent to 1,825.00 per greenback.\par \par \uc2 The Brazilian real ended 0.35 percent stronger at 2.0090 per dollar after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold all of the 20,000 contracts of traditional \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps, which are derivatives contracts designed to strengthen the real.\par \par \uc2 Investors had been on the lookout for a possible \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  since last Thursday, when the real weakened past the level of 2 per dollar - considered a boundary of an informal trading range imposed by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, the real had closed at two-month low of 2.0160 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 Many analysts believe Brazilian policymakers want the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to remain within a range of 1.95-2.0 reais per dollar, slightly stronger than it was at the end of 2012, to avoid inflation pass-through.\par \par \uc2 "On one hand, the government is trying to curb inflation, but at the same time, it is worried about local industry," said Flavio Serrano, an economist with BES Investimento in Sao Paulo, referring to the impact of the exchange rate on exports.\par \par \uc2 "Maybe they found a comfortable level between 1.95 and 2.0 reais," he added.\par \par \uc2 BRAZIL RATES\par \par \uc2 Brazilian interest-rate futures fell sharply on the BM&FBovespa exchange after President Dilma Rousseff said she will not support policies that attempt to curb inflation by slowing down the economy.\par \par \uc2 Investors interpreted her remarks as a sign that policymakers were in no rush to hike the benchmark Selic rate, currently at an all-time low of 7.25 percent.\par \par \uc2 After interest rates fell, Rousseff said her comments were misinterpreted, and that fighting inflation is a "value in itself" in her government.\par \par \uc2 Despite that, interest-rate futures closed lower. Contracts maturing in January 2014 dropped 5 basis points to 7.74 percent.\par \par \uc2 "The most important thing was the president's remark. It was the same as saying that interest rates will not go up," said Waldir Kiel, an economist with H.Commcor brokerage in Sao Paulo.\par \par \uc2 Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  prices 