\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Latam  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  little changed amid Cyprus concerns\b0\par\par\uc2 414 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2013\par 18:20\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Potential for \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  eyed\par \par \uc2 * Mexico peso flat, \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  real dips 0.05 pct\par \par \uc2 (Adds comment, updates prices)\par \par \uc2 By Natalia Cacioli and Bruno Federowski\par \par \uc2 MEXICO CITY, March 25 (Reuters) - Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were little changed on Monday as concerns about a Cyprus bailout deal weighed on demand for riskier assets around the globe.\par \par \uc2 News of a last-minute deal to save Cyprus from a financial meltdown gave way to broader concerns about Europe's banking sector.\par \par \uc2 "The market is oscillating in the wake of such information from Europe," said Luciano Rostagno, chief strategist at \uc2 WestLB\uc2  bank.\par \par \uc2 Brazil's real bid at 2.0105 per dollar, 0.05 percent weaker than Friday's close. It crossed on Thursday the 2-per-dollar mark that many analysts believed to be a boundary of an informal trading range imposed by policymakers.\par \par \uc2 Since then, investors have been wary of a possible \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  losses. Many analysts say the bank is unlikely to allow the real to depreciate too much to avoid inflation pass-through.\par \par \uc2 In Mexico, the peso was flat at 12.3590 per dollar. It gained more than half a percentage point on Friday after higher-than-expected inflation data suggested the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be unable to further lower interest rates in coming months.\par \par \uc2 Mexico's peso is trading near a more than 1-1/2 year high, hit last week, and the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is seen gaining more ground on expectations that the new government will push major economic reforms through a divided Congress this year.\par \par \uc2 "The peso looks very stable because the view remains that foreign investors will keep pouring in. Moreover, with all this issue of global risk, the Mexican economy looks pretty stable," said Rafael Camarena, an economist at Santander in Mexico City.\par \par \uc2 Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  prices at 2230 GMT:\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2                        daily %   YTD %      \par                                 change  change  \par                                                 \par                       Latest                    \par Brazil real             2.0105     -0.05    1.47      \par                                                 \par Mexico peso            12.3590      0.00    4.09      \par                                                 \par Chile peso            472.8000     -0.02    1.25      \par                                                 \par                                                     \par Peru sol                2.5830      0.27   -1.24      \par                                                 \par Argentina peso          5.1150     -0.05   -3.96      \par (interbank)                                       \par                                                 \par Argentina peso          8.2200      3.16  -17.52      \par (parallel)                                        \par                                                 \par                                                     \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Writing by Walter Brandimarte; Additional reporting by Jean Arce in Mexico City; Editing by Andrew Hay and Lisa Shumaker)\f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-LATAM/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 