\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Brazil \highlight\uc2  Real Opens Stronger, Despite Cyprus Vote, On  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Concern\b0\par\par\uc2 416 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2013\par 14:03\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global News Select\par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO--\b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's real strengthened Wednesday as investors shook off worries about Cyprus and grew wary of local \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action to curb losses in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a way to rein in inflation.\par \par \uc2 The real opened at BRL1.9794 to the dollar, according to Tullett Prebon via FactSet, stronger than Tuesday's close at BRL1.9845 to the dollar.\par \par \uc2 The parliament of Cyprus voted against a bailout deal with the euro zone on Tuesday, due in large part to a controversial tax on individual bank deposits. A government delegation has headed to Moscow to try to get money from the Russian government in exchange for stakes in the island's assets.\par \par \uc2 Still, investors were expecting the news, said Paulo Petrassi, a fund manager at Leme Investimentos said.\par \par \uc2 "If news from Cyprus gets worse, we could see the real weaken," he said. "The market is buying dollars, but it's scared that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could enter the market again."\par \par \uc2 The real has been trading between BRL1.95 to BRL2 per dollar for the past two months, with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  holding auctions to contain gains or losses whenever the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  threatens to break past those bounds. After weakening to almost BRL2 per dollar in recent days, the real strengthened again Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Part of that investor concern resulted from \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  comments in the minutes of its latest monetary policy meeting. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  cut the benchmark Selic rate to a record low of 7.25% last year, and despite climbing inflation had said that keeping rates low for a "sufficiently prolonged period," inflation would return to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's target. During its March 6 decision, however, the bank removed that language from its statement, opening the door for rate increases.\par \par \uc2 In minutes of the March 6 meeting, however, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stated the need for "continued caution" in setting monetary policy.\par \par \uc2 "That talk of 'caution' led investors to think they won't raise rates in the short term," Mr Petrassi said. "Since they won't raise rates, they will continue using the real to contain inflation."\par \par \uc2 Although the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has said that it has no ideal rate for the real, and that it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  only to reduce market volatility, investors recognize that allowing the real to weaken too much raises the costs of imports, stoking inflation.\par \par \uc2 Write to Paulo Winterstein at paulo.winterstein@dowjones.com [ 03-20-13 0903ET ]\par \par \uc2 1156 1843   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJON000020130320e93k0004h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
