\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Brazil \highlight\uc2  Real Weakens On  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 447 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2013\par 20:50\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 --Brazilian real ends an active trading day at BRL1.9556 Vs. BRL1.9477\par \par \uc2 --\b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sells reverse-swap contracts, weakening real\par \par \uc2 --Some traders see range of BRL1.95-2.00 to the dollar\par \par \uc2 By Tom Murphy\par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO--The Brazilian real weakened Monday against the U.S. dollar, after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  with an auction of reverse swap contracts.\par \par \uc2 The real ended the trading day at BRL1.9556 to the dollar, weaker than Friday's finish at BRL1.9477, according to Tullett Prebon via FactSet.\par \par \uc2 The real strengthened slightly in early trading, but that brought a swift response from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . In a mid-morning auction, the bank sold so-called reverse-swap contracts. The contracts amount to \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchases of U.S. dollar futures, thus reducing the availability of dollar-hedged contracts for investors and strengthening the dollar. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold the equivalent of $1.35 billion in reverse swaps. "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is showing it doesn't want the dollar to weaken beyond BRL1.95," said Durval Correa, a trader at the Multi Money brokerage.\par \par \uc2 Traders speculated that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be trying to enforce a trading range of BRL1.95 to BRL2.00 to the dollar, with BRL1.95 designed as a limit to protect exporters.\par \par \uc2 In a research note Monday, economists at Banco Itau forecast a year-end value for the dollar at BRL2.00. Economists at RBC Emerging Markets Research, also in a research note, put the current fair value of the Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at BRL1.9580, or very close to Monday's close.\par \par \uc2 The reverse-swap auction also reinforced the widely held view that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will not attempt to use \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation to fight inflation. "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will fight inflation with interest rates," said Mr. Correa.\par \par \uc2 Most economists expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to raise interest rates over the next few months to push down Brazil's worrisome 6.3% inflation rate. The next rate hike could come as early as April, with most economists predicting a quarter-point increase, and some a half-point. The Selic base rate is currently at a historic low of 7.25%. RBC economists predict three quarter-point hikes in a row for Selic from April through July, leaving the rate at 8.0%.\par \par \uc2 In principle, higher interest rates could strengthen the Brazilian real against the U.S. dollar by attracting more foreign investment inflows. But traders said any such effect could be easily counteracted by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as Monday's reverse swaps.\par \par \uc2 Write to Tom Murphy at tom.murphy@dowjones.com [ 11-03-13 1950GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1156 1843   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020130311e93b000r9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
