\par\par \b \uc2 Brazil Real Slightly Stronger On Possible Interest Rate Hikes\b0\par\par\uc2 427 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2013\par 14:41\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 --Brazil real slightly stronger at BRL1.9443 vs. BRL1.9477 Friday\par \par \uc2 --Real gains against dollar ahead of possible interest rate hikes\par \par \uc2 --Economists forecast rate hikes as inflation worries intensify\par \par \uc2 By Tom Murphy\par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO--The Brazilian real was slightly stronger against the U.S. dollar in early trading Monday ahead of possible interest rate hikes that could come as early as April.\par \par \uc2 Early in the session, the real was trading at BRL1.9443 to-the-dollar, stronger against the Friday close of BRL1.9477, according to Tullett Prebon via FactSet.\par \par \uc2 Brazilian inflation is currently running at 6.3%, up from 5.84% at the end of 2012. The 12-month inflation rate has been climbing all year and is now perilously close to the 6.5% ceiling of Brazil's inflation-targeting range.\par \par \uc2 Rising inflation has caused economists to revise their forecasts for interest rates.\par \par \uc2 This week's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  survey, released earlier Monday, showed economists predicting a rise in the Selic base interest rate to 8.0% by the end of the year. The previous forecast by economists was for maintenance of the Selic rate at the current 7.25% throughout 2013.\par \par \uc2 According to many economists, rate hikes could begin as early as the next \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  monetary policy meeting in April.\par \par \uc2 Higher interest rates would tend to strengthen the real against the dollar by attracting more foreign investment inflows.\par \par \uc2 Some \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders are now forecasting a new range for the real against the greenback. Since early this year, market forecasts had focused on a range of about BRL1.95 to BRL2.00 to the dollar. But that view is now changing.\par \par \uc2 "I'm beginning to think we'll see a range more like BRL1.90 to BRL1.95," said Wanderlei Muniz, a trader at southern \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Onnix brokerage. He said \uc2 Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market was unlikely at current levels.\par \par \uc2 Once again, inflation may be orienting \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy. A stronger Brazilian real can help the government combat inflation. At BRL1.90 to-the-dollar, a range of imports from oil products and wheat to mobile phone components become cheaper for Brazilian consumers and businesses than at BRL1.95 or BRL2.00 to-the-dollar.\par \par \uc2 Traders noted that the Brazilian market was largely ignoring international factors Monday, including a decline in most commodities prices and a slightly weaker euro. Investors are focusing on domestic factors, they said.\par \par \uc2 Write to Tom Murphy at tom.murphy@dowjonescom [ 11-03-13 1341GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1156 1843   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020130311e93b000m0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
