\par\par \b \uc2 Brazil's Real Opens Stronger as Governments Tries to Reduce Inflation Worries\b0\par\par\uc2 By Luciana Magalhaes   \par \uc2 365 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Februar 2013\par 14:26\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO--Brazil's real opened stronger against the U.S. dollar Thursday following Brazil's government efforts to reduce inflation worries.\par \par \uc2 The real was trading at BRL1.9667 to the U.S. dollar, stronger than Wednesday's closing price at BRL1.9722, according to Tullett Prebon via FactSet.\par \par \uc2 Brazil's high inflation rate scared away private investors in the past, but the current macro-economic stability in the country favors an increase in private investments, President Dilma Rousseff said Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 The government last year announced a series of plans to attract private financing for the construction or improvement of roads, railways, ports and airports.\par \par \uc2 This year's economic conditions should favor growth, Ms. Rousseff said Wednesday. The situation in Europe is much better, and China appears to have achieved a soft landing for its economic growth, she said.\par \par \uc2 Brazilian officials have been saying in the last few weeks that the country is prioritizing the control of inflation. In the 12-month period through mid-February, the IPCA-15 rose 6.18% versus a 6.02% advance in the same period through mid-January. That remained above the government's official inflation target of 4.5%, but within a tolerance band of plus or minus two percentage points.\par \par \uc2 Rousseff's economic team commitment to controlling prices has created expectations that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could raise interest rates, currently at a record-low 7.25%, despite economic weakness.\par \par \uc2 Earlier in the week, Brazil's Finance Minister Guido Mantega said the country's exchange rate has stabilized to a "favorable" level, lessening the need for \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  last stepped into the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on Feb. 15, when it held a sale of reverse-swap contracts to keep the real trading weaker than the BRL1.95 level per dollar.\par \par \uc2 The minister also said \uc2 Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  can adjust monetary police if it believes it's necessary. "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has the independence to raise interest rates if it thinks necessary," he added.\par \par \uc2 Write to Luciana Magalhaes at luciana.magalhaes@dowjones.com [ 28-02-13 1326GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1156 1843   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020130228e92s000ua\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
