\par\par \b \uc2 Brazil Makes Inflation Control A Priority\b0\par\par\uc2 596 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Februar 2013\par 17:45\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global News Select\par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK--Brazil's exchange rate has stabilized to a "favorable" level, lessening the need for \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Finance Minister Guido Mantega said Tuesday.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the real, has fluctuated in a wide range over the past 12 months, largely driven by \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market as well as capital controls introduced by the government.\par \par \uc2 This month, however, the real has traded in a relatively narrow range, between BRL1.95 to BRL2 per dollar. It traded at BRL1.9855 Tuesday afternoon in New York.\par \par \uc2 In an interview with The Wall Street Journal, Mr. Mantega said that the exchange rate is now less volatile and at "a level that we can live with."\par \par \uc2 His comments reflect a shift for Mr. Mantega, who was one of the first to use the phrase "\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  war" in 2010 to refer to a widespread effort among global policy makers to weaken their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to make exports more competitive. \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been one of the more frequent \b \highlight22\uc2 interveners\b0 \highlight\uc2  in its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Mr. Mantega said Tuesday that \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  was sitting out the latest round of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  war.\par \par \uc2 "Since we've been able to bring the exchange rate to a favorable level for the country, at this time we're not suffering what would've been characterized as a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  war," he said, adding that the real is close to a "balanced situation."\par \par \uc2 That's part of the reason Brazilian authorities have been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  less and less, and Mr. Mantega said that new measures are "not necessary."\par \par \uc2 Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  last stepped into the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on Feb. 15, when it held a sale of reverse-swap contracts to keep the real trading weaker than the BRL1.95 level per dollar.\par \par \uc2 Now the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is at a level where Brazil can still be competitive but doesn't add to inflationary pressures, Mr. Mantega said, adding that it has stabilized "roughly" to the area of BRL1.90-BRL2.10 per dollar. Both he and \uc2 Brazil \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President \uc2 Alexandre Tombini\uc2  have attributed the recent rise in inflation partly to the real's sharp depreciation in 2012, when it fell about 10% against the dollar. Consumer prices have accelerated recently, with annualized inflation reaching 6.2% in mid-February.\par \par \uc2 Some analysts have said that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have been too aggressive in its decision to cut its benchmark interest rate to a historical low of 7.25% in 2012, given persistent inflation in Brazil.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Mantega, however, defended the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , calling its series of rate cuts to 7.25% the "correct" move.\par \par \uc2 Now, with inflationary pressures still clouding Brazil's recovery outlook, a number of analysts expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to start raising interest rates this year. There has been some speculation that the government would try to pressure the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  not to raise rates, as the record low level is seen as a major achievement for President Dilma Rousseff.\par \par \uc2 But Mr. Mantega said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  can adjust monetary policy as it sees fit.\par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has the independence to raise interest rates if it thinks necessary," he added.\par \par \uc2 He, however, believes that inflation is still on a downward trend this year, in part because the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has stabilized after last year's sharp depreciation.\par \par \uc2 "Inflation will be better this year than last year," he added.\par \par \uc2 --Brian Baskin and Paulo Trevisani contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Erin McCarthy at erin.mccarthy@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 Subscribe to WSJ: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://online.wsj.com?mod=djnwires"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://online.wsj.com?mod=djnwires }}}\uc2  [ 02-26-13 1145ET ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJON000020130226e92q0006f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
