\par\par \b \uc2 DJ Brazil's Economic Policy Becomes Guesswork For Traders\b0\par\par\uc2 784 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Februar 2013\par 19:21\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2013, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 --Brazil's economic policy becomes guesswork for traders\par \par \uc2 --Investors struggle to play Brazil as policymakers seem to diverge\par \par \uc2 --Markets long for a steady hand on Brazilian policy making \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Paulo Trevisani \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investors may take courage from Brazil's projected economic pickup this year but they are still spooked by divergent remarks coming from policy makers and their effects on the country's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the real, as authorities juggle the need to fuel the economy with that of keeping inflation at bay.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 For the time being, anyway, investors are hanging on to the idea that authorities will keep the real in a relatively tight range between BRL1.95 and BRL2.00 to the U.S. dollar.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The seesawing of the exchange rate in recent months has highlighted the difficulties of trying to manage a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  that is still considered to be free-floating despite the introduction of targeted capital controls over the last year or so.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Investors still like Brazil," said Tony Volpon, head of Emerging Markets Research Americas at Nomura Securities in New York. But the country now has "adverse movements while other countries are doing well," partly because of mixed signals sent by the Brazilian government, he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Earlier this month, after a period of appreciation of the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  against the U.S. dollar, a comment from Finance Minister \f28 \uc2 \f2 Guido Mantega\f28 \uc2 \f2  briefly led market participants to believe the exchange rate could be headed toward BRL1.85. The minister then clarified that the government has no target and won't let the real appreciate much.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Since then, some traders have adopted BRL1.95 to BRL2.00 as the closest thing to an unofficial target range.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On Friday, Mr. Mantega again shook up trading desks when he said interest rates--and not the exchange rate--remain Brazil's main weapon against inflation. This drew some applause as it suggested the government wouldn't prevent the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  from raising interest rates to combat inflation, which accelerated to 6.15% in January. The benchmark Selic rate target is at a record low 7.25%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The more-hawkish stance is very welcome," said Barclays Capital analyst Marcelo Salomon.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But for some, a hawkish stance is far from enough.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "You don't know what they want," said Nomura's Mr. Volpon, referring to Brazilian policy makers. "They don't care so much about predictability."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 It doesn't help that the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Brazilian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is part of the Finance Ministry, which makes Mr. Mantega the boss of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  President \f28 \uc2 \f2 Alexandre Tombini\f28 \uc2 \f2 . In addition, Brazilian President \f28 \uc2 \f2 Dilma Rousseff\f28 \uc2 \f2 , an economist by training, is more hands-on regarding economic policy than her predecessor, Luiz Inacio Lula da Silva.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 While \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Brazil\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has always had a reputation for strong government \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the economy, its policy-making hasn't always been such a mystery for investors who now think its hand has become more unsteady.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Since controlling hyperinflation in the mid-1990s, Brazilian authorities have gained investors' confidence by keeping a clear anti-inflation stance, even when it meant raising interest rates to 45% in 1999.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Now, prices are creeping up amid strong income growth, with unemployment just above 4%. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 --which just a few years ago would have been certain to raise interest rates to contain inflation--has the markets trying to guess what it will do.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The goodwill [towards Brazil] has vanished," said Goldman Sachs chief Latin America economist Alberto Ramos, "and it has migrated to Mexico, which is more orderly."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Smaller Latin American economies such as Peru, Colombia and Chile have shown more consistency in their policies. While investors tend to like that, they haven't flown en masse to those markets because they are still small. But Mexico, Latin America's second-largest economy after Brazil, seems to be benefiting, at least in its stock market, given investor concerns regarding Brazil, Mr. Ramos added.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Most economists are now forecasting modest economic growth above 3% in Brazil this year, with inflation near 6.5%, the top of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's target range. An increase in interest rates is predicted for late this year or early 2014.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Fernando Sotelino, an adjunct professor at Columbia University's School of International and Public Affairs, said Brazil needs to "come back to reality" after years of growth fueled mainly by global demand for its commodities and promote reforms to reduce the cost of doing business in the country.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "There is an obsession with the short term," he said, but Brazil needs to start building long-term credibility.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Write to Paulo Trevisani at paulo.trevisani@wsj.com.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 February 19, 2013 13:21 ET (18:21 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020130219e92j005gp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
