\par\par \b \uc2 CORRECTED-WRAPUP 2-\uc2 Brazil's  \highlight22\uc2 central bank \highlight\uc2  points to new  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  trading band\b0\par\par\uc2 893 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Februar 2013\par 23:35\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Corrects 16th paragraph to say reverse swap auction mimics \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchase of dollars, not sale of dollars)\par \par \uc2 * Real initially rallies as government seen favoring strong \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 * \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to halt \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  gains\par \par \uc2 * Mantega suggests government willing to accept stronger real\par \par \uc2 * Analysts see new trading band of 1.95-2.05 per dollar\par \par \uc2 By Walter Brandimarte\par \par \uc2 RIO DE JANEIRO, Feb 8 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to halt a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rally on Friday, setting the boundaries of a new informal trading band that government officials hope will help curb inflation without hurting exporters.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  followed a week of intense volatility in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market as investors scrambled to figure out how policymakers were adjusting Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy. The real, which stabilized and closed the week at 1.9715 per dollar, is now likely to enter the traditional Carnival lull that will halt Brazilian markets for most of next week.\par \par \uc2 The real was rallying for a second day early on Friday after comments by a number of policymakers, including Finance Minister Guido Mantega, suggested the government was ready to accept a stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cheapen the price of imported goods and put a lid on inflation.\par \par \uc2 In an interview with Reuters on Thursday night, Mantega said the real, which last week gained past the level of 2 per dollar for the first time in seven months, "has found a reasonable floating band."\par \par \uc2 The minister, who has always defended a weaker real to support exporters, sounded more amenable to a stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as he cited the 1.85-per-dollar threshold as an example of a level that would not be allowed.\par \par \uc2 His remark came shortly after \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief \uc2 Alexandre Tombini\uc2  told a Brazilian newspaper that he was worried about rising consumer prices in the short term, comments that many interpreted as a sign the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was favoring a stronger real to curb inflation.\par \par \uc2 In the interview, Mantega sounded less concerned about inflation than Tombini, but the two appeared to be converging on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy. Economists say the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  favors a slightly stronger real to help slow inflation, whereas finance ministry officials have argued that a firmer \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could spur investment by making it cheaper for companies to import capital goods and machinery.\par \par \uc2 Just last week, Mantega and Tombini appeared to be on a collision course when the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the opposite direction to halt a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  sell-off, also triggered by Mantega.\par \par \uc2 Taken together, Mantega's and Tombini's now converging comments were interpreted as a green light for a stronger real, driving the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  more than 1 percent higher in early trading on Friday.\par \par \uc2 The real had already gained about 0.8 percent on Thursday on growing speculation that the government would use the exchange rate to curb inflation, which is dangerously close to the ceiling of a government target.\par \par \uc2 Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had lost nearly 30 percent against the dollar between July 2011 and last November, but gained about 8 percent since then.\par \par \uc2 TOO FAST?\par \par \uc2 Signs that the government was not happy about the speed of appreciation of the real in the past couple of days were already evident early on Friday, when a source on President Dilma Rousseff's economic team told Reuters gains had been "somewhat exaggerated."\par \par \uc2 After piercing the level of 2 per dollar last week, market analysts estimated the real's informal trading range, which for months was set at 2.0 to 2.1 per dollar, was shifting to around 1.95 to 2.05.\par \par \uc2 Investors, eager to test that theory, pushed the real to a nine-month high of 1.9510 per dollar on Friday morning.\par \par \uc2 That was when the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 , offering to sell as much as 30,000 reverse \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps - derivative contracts that emulate the purchase of dollars in the futures market and are often used by policymakers to weaken the real.\par \par \uc2 "They seem to be doing two things: one is to try to slow down the appreciation of the real, but they also seem to be putting a boundary for the real at 1.95 per dollar, at least for now," said Enrique Alvarez, chief of Latin America research at IDEAglobal in New York.\par \par \uc2 Further adding to the notion of a new trading band around the level of 2 per dollar, Development Minister Fernando Pimentel told Valor Economico newspaper that a real between 1.96 and 2.05 per dollar would be acceptable to industry.\par \par \uc2 Some analysts wondered whether the government was clumsily trying to stem \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility, a factor that some economists say muddies Brazil's investment climate.\par \par \uc2 "While investors may be skeptical about Brazil's intentions near term, a solid and ongoing commitment to a stable real, instead of a weaker real, may end up marking its return to being a favored emerging market trade," \uc2 Brown Brothers Harriman\uc2 's analysts said in a research note.\par \par \uc2 "We're not there yet, not with all these policy flip-flops," they added.\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Natalia Cacioli in Sao Paulo, Alonso Soto in Brasilia; editing by Phil Berlowitz, Dan Grebler, Jeb Blount)\par \par \uc2 BRAZIL-\b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCY\b0 \highlight\uc2 / (WRAPUP 2) (CORRECTED) | ABN | D | E | RBN | M | NAT | ABX | BNX | FUN | U | C | O | MTL | GRO | SOF | OIL | RNP | DNP | PTD   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020130208e928001l9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
