\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Latam stocks up despite Brazilian  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  worries\b0\par\par\uc2 576 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Februar 2013\par 16:56\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Brazil's Linx, Mexico's Sanborns make market debuts\par \par \uc2 * Brazil markets to close from Monday to 1 p.m. Wednesday\par \par \uc2 * Brazil Bovespa rises 0.21 pct, Mexico IPC up 0.16 pct\par \par \uc2 By Danielle Assalve\par \par \uc2 SAO PAULO, Feb 8 (Reuters) - Latin American stocks ended slightly up on Friday but Brazilian gains were limited because of concerns that a strengthening real will hurt exports.\par \par \uc2 Despite the modest gains, Brazil's Bovespa index fell 3.1 percent for the week, its worst weekly performance since November last year.\par \par \uc2 Analysts said the poor Bovespa showing reflected larger concerns about the Brazilian economy. Earlier this week Brazil announced that consumer prices jumped in January by the most in almost eight years, pushing annual inflation up to 6.15 percent, and scaring off risk-averse investors.\par \par \uc2 On Friday, \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to halt a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rally, setting the boundaries of a new informal trading band that government officials hope will help curb inflation without hurting exporters.\par \par \uc2 "There is a perception that Brazil is in macroeconomic disarray and this affects the outlook for companies," said Silvio Campos Neto, an economist at Tendencias Consultoria in Sao Paulo. "Investors want a more stable environment for investing."\par \par \uc2 The Bovespa ended the day up 0.21 percent at 58,497.83 points after data showed Chinese exports grew more than expected in January. China is Brazil's biggest trade partner.\par \par \uc2 Nonetheless, market participants said optimism over the Chinese data was limited because Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the real , strengthened sharply in recent sessions, hitting its strongest level in nine months on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 A stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  can help curb inflation because it makes\par \par \uc2 imports cheaper for local consumers. It hurts exporting firms, though, especially in a commodities powerhouse like Brazil, because it makes their goods more expensive abroad.\par \par \uc2 In Brazil, state oil giant \uc2 Petrobras\uc2  drove gains, adding 1.37 percent, while shares of \uc2 Linx SA\uc2 , Brazil's largest maker of management software for retailers, rose 18.52 percent in their first day of trading on the Bovespa.\par \par \uc2 The firm's initial public offering priced at the top of the suggested range, according to a filing on Wednesday. A fund manager who participated in the IPO attributed strong demand to Linx's established name in Brazil.\par \par \uc2 Brazilian markets will be closed on Monday through Wednesday for carnival.\par \par \uc2 Mexico's IPC index was up 0.16 percent, but down 1.48 for the week.\par \par \uc2 Shares in the off-IPC Grupo Sanborns, the Mexican retailer and coffee chain owned by \uc2 Carlos Slim\uc2 , saw a rocky market debut, starting the day up before falling off to end at 28.46 pesos/share, slightly up on the 28 peso/share list price.\par \par \uc2 Chile's bourse was nearly unchanged. The IPSA ended the week up 0.5 percent.\par \par \uc2 Latin America's key stock indexes at 2142 GMT:\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par   \par Stock indexes                                  Pct \par                         Latest              chang\par                                                 e\par                                                  \par MSCI LatAm                       3,902.11      0.14\par                                             \par Brazil Bovespa