\par\par \b \uc2 Brazil's Industrial Outlook Still Unclear After Slow 2012--Trade Group\b0\par\par\uc2 By Gerald Jeffris   \par \uc2 505 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Februar 2013\par 17:40\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global News Select\par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 BRASILIA--The outlook for a strong recovery of Brazilian industry in coming months remains unclear following signs of deceleration of activity late in 2012, officials at the National Confederation of Industries, or CNI, said Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 CNI economists said that they still expect a "gradual recovery" of Brazilian activity ahead following a "lost year" in 2012, but this will depend on a resumption of investment, which remains uncertain.\par \par \uc2 "Although there were some signs of improvement during 2012, they weren't with the intensity needed to characterize a recovery of industrial activity," said CNI Chief Economist Flavio Castelo Branco.\par \par \uc2 The CNI's latest industrial data released Wednesday showed that use of installed capacity declined by 0.6 percentage points over 2012 to end the year at 80.9%, while hours worked declined 1.5 percentage points.\par \par \uc2 On the upside, industrial sales rose 2.4 percentage points during the year. However, this likely reflected adjusted inventories as companies sold previously produced goods.\par \par \uc2 "There's no pattern of clarity in the latest data," Mr. Castelo Branco said. "We need to see some more months of continued improvement to determine whether we're in a recovery."\par \par \uc2 Brazilian industry, he said, is holding out hope for reduced costs and growing productivity brought by recent government initiatives to cut payroll taxes and lower electricity prices. But the full impact from these moves may only be determined late in the first quarter, he said.\par \par \uc2 However, local companies remain concerned about factors that continue to weigh against a resumption of activity, including still-sluggish and competitive conditions in global markets, and recent unexpected \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strengthening.\par \par \uc2 "The prospect that we had was that the exchange rate would show more stability in 2013 at around BRL2.10 to the dollar," said Mr. Castelo Branco. "But recent actions by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  brought appreciation, so this represents some uncertainty for the year ahead."\par \par \uc2 Amid concerns over accelerated inflation, \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  periodically in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market over recent weeks in an effort to boost the strength of the real. As a result, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moved from levels near BRL2.05 to the dollar to stronger than the BRL2.00 per dollar mark.\par \par \uc2 A further hurdle for increasing local productivity, the CNI said, is a low supply of qualified labor for new projects as Brazil's unemployment rate tests historic lows.\par \par \uc2 "If this was a problem during a slowdown, imagine if there's any growth," said CNI economist Marcelo D'Avila. "We need to watch this because it could affect costs."\par \par \uc2 In the face of these factors and a still uncertain recovery of demand, CNI officials said there was little guarantee local industries would seek to swiftly expand their \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  this year.\par \par \uc2 "If there's idle capacity that can still be used, then industries will be less inclined to invest for new production," Mr. Castelo Branco said.\par \par \uc2 Write to Gerald Jeffris at gerald.jeffris@dowjones.com [ 02-06-13 1140ET ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJON000020130206e9260005f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
