\par\par \b \uc2 Inflation Worries Keep Market Guessing on  \highlight22\uc2 Brazil \highlight\uc2  Real Level\b0\par\par\uc2 613 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Januar 2013\par 19:06\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 --\b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Monday may signal growing inflation concerns\par \par \uc2 --Real trades stronger than BRL2.00 to-the-dollar for first time in months\par \par \uc2 --Markets pondering whether stronger level for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be permanent\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Jeffrey T. Lewis \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BRASILIA--\f28 \uc2 \f2 Brazil's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  allowed the Brazilian real to strengthen past the BRL2.00 to-the-dollar level this week for the first time since July of 2012, signaling growing concern over inflation and leaving market participants guessing as to what range the bank now wants for the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  spent months defending an unofficial range of BRL2.00-to-BRL2.10 per dollar, but on Monday it sold $1.9 billion in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  swap contracts even as the real was strengthening against the dollar.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Sale of the swaps, which increase dollar availability in the futures market, tended to accelerate the real's strengthening trend Monday, with the real closing at exactly BRL2.00 per greenback, gaining from the Friday close of BRL2.0285. As of mid-afternoon Tuesday, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  had gained even more, trading at BRL1.9873 to-the-dollar, according to Tullett Prebon via FactSet.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Market participants see Monday's sale of swap contracts as confirmation that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is now more concerned about price pressures than economic growth.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The government made a cost-benefit decision between raising interest rates and letting the real appreciate, and they chose the latter," explained Ilan Solot, an analyst at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Brown Brothers Harriman\f28 \uc2 \f2  in London. "That's an admission that inflation is pressuring and could get worse."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A stronger real could help to bring down prices. Brazilian inflation is now running at a worrisome annual rate of 6.02%, well above the government target for this year of 4.5%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "A stronger real will help the government combat inflation by bringing down prices of imports," said Lorenzo Rodrigo, a partner in Sao Paulo's Torre brokerage. Official inflation statistics are especially sensitive to essential imports such as wheat and fuel.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  spent the last months of 2011 and most of 2012 cutting its benchmark interest rate by more than five percentage points, leaving it at a record low of 7.25%, in an effort to help spur flagging economic growth.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 During the same period, the government implemented a series of measures to encourage a weaker real and boost exports. The pace of the country's economic growth had slowed from 7.5% in 2010 to 2.7% in 2011 and about 1% last year.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But the government is predicting faster economic growth this year, in a range of 3.5% to 4.0%. That puts the focus back on controlling price increases in a country with a long history of hyperinflation.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 After Monday's auction of swap contracts and the so-far unopposed move of the real past the BRL2.00 to-the-dollar level Tuesday, investors and traders are now pondering what the new unofficial range will be for the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "I'm thinking it will be BRL1.95-to-BRL2.00, but it's really early to be guessing at this point," said Reginaldo Siaca, a manager at the Pioneer brokerage in Sao Paulo. "The market is readjusting."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Mr. Solot at BBH agrees with Mr. Siaca's guess on the range, though Flavia Cattan-Naslausky at RBS said she sees a range of BRL1.98 to BRL2.02 and Alessio de Longhis of Oppenheimer Funds expects a wider range of BRL1.95 to BRL2.05.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 --Prabha Natarajan in New York contributed to this report.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Write to Jeffrey T. Lewis at jeffrey.lewis@dowjones.com [ 29-01-13 1806GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020130129e91t000ul\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
