\par\par \b \uc2 EMERGING MARKETS-Mexican peso weakens on rate cut fears, real jumps\b0\par\par\uc2 692 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Januar 2013\par 17:20\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Brazil real up 1.5 pct on \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swap rollover\par \par \uc2 * Investors bet Brazil's move reflects inflation concerns\par \par \uc2 * Mexico's peso loses 0.45 pct as Carstens bolsters rate cut bets\par \par \uc2 (Updates prices, adds details)\par \par \uc2 By Jean Arce and Alexandra Alper\par \par \uc2 RIO DE JANEIRO, Jan 28 (Reuters) - The Mexican peso lost ground for the second trading session in a row on Monday after comments by the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor bolstered bets that Latin America's No. 2 economy is eyeing a rate cut.\par \par \uc2 But Brazil's real gained 1.5 percent, bucking losses in other Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  signaled it would favor a stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to fight inflation.\par \par \uc2 The Mexican peso lost 0.45 percent to 12.7652 per dollar after \uc2 Mexico's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  head \uc2 Agustin Carstens\uc2  said the peso is trading at "a relatively competitive level."\par \par \uc2 His comments backed the bank's statement earlier this month that it could move to cut rates if inflation continues to cool and economic growth flags, dropping its threat to tighten policy.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's communique "takes support away from the (Mexican peso), which is faced with the expectation of a possible benchmark rate cut in the near future and much more dovish minutes than in the last communique," Banorte-IXE analysts said in a note to clients on Monday.\par \par \uc2 The peso has slipped about 1.33 percent from a 10 month high notched on Jan. 17, the day before the communique's release, and is still weaker than it was before the global financial crisis.\par \par \uc2 "It's going to be a cautious week for financial markets because of (U.S.) economic data that is coming out," said Rafael Camarena, an economist at \uc2 Banco Santander\uc2 , in reference to the U.S. preliminary fourth quarter GDP results, a \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  statement, and an employment report slated for later this week.\par \par \uc2 Camarena added that the peso would likely strengthen if the data is positive.\par \par \uc2 In Brazil, speculation that the government now wants a real around 2 per dollar - the latest in a series of changes of heart by policymakers - grew after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  announced on Monday that it was rolling over all of the 37,000 traditional \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps that expire on Feb. 1.\par \par \uc2 Those contracts, which mimic the sale of dollars in the futures market, were originally sold by the bank to boost liquidity in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market at the end of the year, a period when greenbacks traditionally are scarcer.\par \par \uc2 The swap rollover "is a good indication that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is really becoming more tolerant to a stronger real, at least in the short term," said Flavia Cattan-Naslausky, a strategist with \uc2 RBS Securities\uc2  in Stamford, Connecticut. "I believe that is related to a deterioration in inflation."\par \par \uc2 The real closed at 2.0006 per dollar, 1.5 percent stronger from Friday's close. Investors could soon try to take the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  past 2 per dollar -- a mark it has not crossed since early July, when the government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in order to boost exports.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's benchmark consumer inflation rate closed 2012 at 5.84 percent, well above the center of a government target of 4.5 percent, but still within a tolerance range of 2 percentage points. Prices continued to climb in the beginning of 2013 as items such as food and personal expenses rose.\par \par \uc2 Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  prices at 23:55 GMT:\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2                          daily %    YTD %\par                                   change   change\par                          Latest           \par Brazil real                2.0006      1.5     1.97\par                                           \par Mexico peso               12.7652    -0.45     0.78\par                                           \par Chile peso               473.1000    -0.27     1.18\par                                           \par Colombia peso           1780.7300    -0.07    -0.83\par                                           \par Peru sol                   2.5600    -0.08    -0.35\par                                           \par Argentina peso             4.9700    -0.05    -1.16\par (interbank)                                 \par                                           \par Argentina peso             7.5800     0.53   -10.55\par (parallel)                                  \par                                           \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (With reporting and writing by Walter Brandimarte, Editing by \f28 \uc2 \f2 Leslie Adler\f28 \uc2 \f2  and Phil Berlowitz)\f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-LATAM/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  (UPDATE 1) | ABN | D | E | RBN | M | NAT | C | O | U | MTL | GRO | SOF | OIL | RNP | DNP | PTD   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020130131e91s001l1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw145\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2