\par\par \uc2 FX Horizons\par \b \uc2 As Rhetoric Heats Up,  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  War Risks Rise\b0\par\par\uc2 By Michael J. Casey   \par \uc2 824 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Januar 2013\par 01:41\par \uc2 Dow Jones Top Global Market Stories\par \uc2 DJTGMS\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2013 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 Guido Mantega is finally getting some company.\par \par \uc2 In September 2010, the Brazilian finance minister caused a mini commotion when he declared that the world was gripped by a "\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  war." Mr. Mantega complained that the hyper-loose monetary policies pursued by advanced countries, coupled with the exporter-backed exchange-rate manipulations of China, were deflecting unwanted "hot money" into emerging market countries and forcing them to take retaliatory \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -weakening measures of their own. Those remarks, which evoked the specter of the vicious cycle of beggar-thy-neighbor policies that exacerbated the Great Depression, triggered much debate and even provided the title for a bestselling book by investment banker James G. Rickards. But at the time economic pundits mostly dismissed it as an alarmist reading of what was really just a post-crisis policy realignment.\par \par \uc2 Two and a half years later, Mr. Mantega's bandwagon is getting some fellow riders. With the yen having lost 13% of its value against the dollar in four months as the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  has caved into demands from new Prime Minister \uc2 Shinzo Abe\uc2  for looser monetary policy and with the \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2  well into a new round of open-ended "quantitative easing," \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -war warnings are coming thick and fast. And they're coming from figures that might be expected to take a more sober view of the world.\par \par \uc2 In separate speeches Tuesday, outgoing Bank of England Gov. Mervyn King expressed fear that the global financial system was facing a wave of competitive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  depreciations and Deutsche Bundesbank President \uc2 Jens Weidmann\uc2  chastised the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  for policies that "could lead to an increasingly politicized exchange rate." That followed similar warnings this month about the fallout from Japan's actions by St. Louis Fed President \uc2 James Bullard\uc2  and \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  board member Benoit Coeure, and a complaint from Jean-Claude Juncker, Luxembourg's prime minister who heads the Eurogroup of finance ministers, that the euro was "dangerously high."\par \par \uc2 Emerging market governments are also piping up. Among others, Russian and South Korean central bankers have warned that other countries may follow Japan's lead and weaken their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 By the reckoning of the \uc2 Royal Bank of Scotland\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy team, 2013 could see this current round of "\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fracas morph into something more internationally combustible." So, in preparation for a global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  war, they came up with a matrix showing which central banks have the most "intent" and/or "capacity" to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The top scorers on both counts: the banks of Chile, Thailand, Malaysia and, somewhat surprisingly, Sweden.\par \par \uc2 It might be tempting to dismiss all this as mere hyperbole. The situation is far less severe than the brutal \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  wars of the 1930s, which were intrinsically linked to trade restrictions that governments used to protect their national industries and which drained world trade to a trickle. For now at least, governments are too respectful of the \uc2 World Trade Organization\uc2  to go down that route.\par \par \uc2 But a strongly held view exists that extreme monetary easing and proactive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategies, which lie outside the purview of \uc2 WTO\uc2  regulations, represent protectionism by another name. Either way, we are at risk of a series of tit-for-tat measures doing great harm to the global economy. The end result could be an all-out trade war or a wholesale loss of confidence in paper money, along with surging inflation.\par \par \uc2 By speaking about this threat, policymakers often justify their own bad behavior. Mr. Mantega might have taken a holier-than-thou stance when he attacked the Fed and the \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2  but it gave him an excuse to launch \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's own \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy, including a tax on capital inflows, to weaken its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the real.\par \par \uc2 In a similar vein, the BOE's Mr. King accompanied his cautionary remarks Tuesday with statements that the pound needed a 25% depreciation to rebalance the struggling UK economy and that his \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would resort to more quantitative easing if needed.\par \par \uc2 Such statements do little to breed mutual trust among trading nations. Countries need to acknowledge the negative international impact of their policies and, where possible, foreswear actions that undermine global trade. (The Fed, for one, could simply acknowledge the self-evident truth that quantitative easing weakens the dollar and creates imbalances in the global economy-even if its intent is to boost domestic rather than external demand.) Only then can countries come together in the mutual interest of a free-floating \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  system that enhances world commerce and promotes shared prosperity.\par \par \uc2 (Michael Casey is managing editor for the Americas at DJ \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Trader, a \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  news service from Dow Jones Newswires and The Wall Street Journal. His new book on the global financial system, "The Unfair Trade," was published in May. Write to Michael Casey at michael.j.casey@dowjones.com.)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJTGMS1120130124e91o00017\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw427\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6405\pichgoal600
