\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Rally Hems In Latin America\b0\par\par\uc2 By Graciela Ibanez and Anthony Harrup   \par \uc2 698 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Januar 2013\par 01:41\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 The recent rally in Latin American \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  has exporters in the region renewing calls for \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and government action to slow the appreciation they fear will blunt their competitive edge at a time of slowing demand for their exports.\par \par \uc2 In Chile, Colombia and Peru, the dollars are pouring back in as global economic jitters have subsided and U.S. investors again seek the higher returns on investments that emerging markets offer.\par \par \uc2 A strong local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and high labor and energy costs are creating "a perfect storm" for exporters, said Wilfred Leigh, chief executive at Chilean vintner Bethwines.\par \par \uc2 "One solution would be to increase our wine prices but, as Europe is our largest market, if we raise prices nobody there would buy our wines," Mr. Leigh said. "Europeans are undergoing their own crisis."\par \par \uc2 Brazilian exporters blamed their strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the real, for last year's drop in the country's trade surplus as exports declined 5% and imports fell only 1%.\par \par \uc2 "We're facing price competition not only in export markets but also in the domestic market," said Flavio Castelo Branco, chief economist of the Brazilian Confederation of Industries.\par \par \uc2 Calls by exporters for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Chile picked up in recent weeks when the peso reached a three-month high against the U.S. dollar, twice closing around 470 Chilean pesos to the dollar, a psychological level at which investors estimate the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 . That is where the peso was when the bank launched a yearlong \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  program in January 2011.\par \par \uc2 Others see renewed \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as unlikely. They say the cause of the current appreciation is dollar weakness partly tied to the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 's largess.\par \par \uc2 "At 470, investors buy dollars and the peso depreciates," said Sergio Tricio, head of research at \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  Chile.\par \par \uc2 Chilean \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Rodrigo Vergara gave no clues in an interview as to whether the bank would take action to curb peso strength. "Some sectors have benefited from significant price increases abroad," he said. "Others have a more complicated outlook. So naturally, some manufacturing or agricultural sectors have been much more affected than others and they speak out against this."\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  across Latin America has varied, with \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2  the most active in managing swings and Mexico among the most hands-off.\par \par \uc2 Nomura Latin America strategist Benito Berber said \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation is bound to be an issue in the region in 2013, noting recent statements by Colombian officials about weakening the Colombian peso.\par \par \uc2 Moves to weaken local \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  could lead investors to shop around when choosing where in the region to park funds, since depreciation of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  reduces the gains on local-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bonds. "It's something they take into account," Mr. Berber said.\par \par \uc2 Mexico's peso is one of the most-traded emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and as a result it is among the most vulnerable to swings in investor mood.\par \par \uc2 The Mexican peso last week reached its strongest level since September 2011, moving below 12.60 pesos to the dollar. Finance Minister Luis Videgaray said the government and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will maintain the policy of accumulating foreign reserves as a "prudential measure" against bouts of international market volatility.\par \par \uc2 Colombia's Finance Minister \uc2 Mauricio Cardenas\uc2  shrugged off cries for greater action to curtail Colombian peso strength, saying the recent appreciation is "transitory" and that the exchange rate would soon return to 1,800 Colombian pesos to the dollar.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Colombian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  currently buys at least $20 million a day, and the Finance Ministry makes discretionary dollar purchases.\par \par \uc2 "The problem goes beyond simply buying dollars...it requires more aggressive and more audacious measures," including possibly capital controls, said Augusto Solano, who heads a Colombian flower exporters association.\par \par \uc2 Costa Rica is planning just that. The government this week proposed to Congress temporary measures including stiff taxes on financial gains by foreign investors that are transferred out of the country. President Laura Chinchilla last week described short-term capital inflows as "genuine weapons of mass destruction" for receiving economies.\par \par \uc2 --\uc2 Tom Murphy\uc2  in Sao Paulo, Darcy Crowe in Bogota and Carolina Pica in Santiago contributed to this article [ 01-22-13 1941ET ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020130123e91n0000h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
