\par\par \b \uc2 Brazil's Real Closes Slightly Stronger in Slow Trading\b0\par\par\uc2 By Paul Kiernan   \par \uc2 384 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Januar 2013\par 20:34\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2013 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 RIO DE JANEIRO--The Brazilian real strengthened slightly against the dollar in slow trading Monday, as little news came out of the local market and investors abroad digested mixed signals for the global economy.\par \par \uc2 The real exited active trading at BRL2.0309 to the dollar, according to Tullett Prebon via FactSet, compared with BRL2.0331 at Friday's close.\par \par \uc2 U.S. equities, which Brazilian assets often track, were roughly flat as President \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  and congressional Republicans reiterated their positions for upcoming debt negotiations, highlighting one of the key threats to stability in financial markets. Political impasse could force the U.S. to default on its obligations in mid- to late February unless Congress authorizes an increase in the country's $16.4 trillion borrowing limit.\par \par \uc2 On the other hand, the \uc2 Organization for Economic Cooperation and Development\uc2  released a report painting an improved picture for the U.S. and other major economies, while a top official at the U.S. Federal Reserve predicted monetary stimulus will continue into the second half of 2013.\par \par \uc2 The real traded steadily through it all. Market participants see little chance of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciating beyond BRL2.02 to the dollar or weakening beyond BRL2.05, as \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's government and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to limit large fluctuations in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  during recent months.\par \par \uc2 Brazil's real experienced some volatility during the final weeks of 2012 as companies bought dollars to import goods for the holiday shopping season, repatriate profits and pay dividends overseas, among other year-end transactions. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traded as weak as BRL2.14 to the dollar before \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  brought it gradually back toward current levels.\par \par \uc2 Joao Querino de Santos, a trader at J. Alves brokerage in Sao Paulo, said business has been slow so far this year.\par \par \uc2 "It's very calm, kind of weird," Mr. Santos said, adding he and his colleagues have been arriving in the office later and going home earlier than they were a few weeks ago. "They say the year starts and Brazil only gets up to speed after Carnival [holidays in February], so maybe things will heat up afterward."\par \par \uc2 Write to Paul Kiernan at paul.kiernan@dowjones.com [ 14-01-13 1934GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1156 1843   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020130114e91e000up\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
