\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  Steps Into Swaps Futures\b0\par\par\uc2 1,288 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Dezember 2012\par 15:32\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2012 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 9:31 EST - \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2 's (MS) decision to invest in Eris Exchange and become a key liquidity provider to the platform comes as futures and new hybrids called "swap futures" are expected to win out over complex swaps that will be expensive to trade under new global derivatives rules. Eris has a proprietary "swap futures" contract for interest-rate hedging that has seen struggling volumes since its launch, and now has to compete with a newly launched swap future in rates from more-prominent provider \uc2 CME Group\uc2 . The agreement between the bank and the exchange shows the length platform owners will go to in order to ensure dealer support as the rules shake up derivatives trade, and for \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  it's an endorsement of the expected shift from swaps to futures. (katy.burne@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 9:30 EST - Carnival's (CCL) fiscal 4Q yield falls less than company predicted and EPS beats guidance by 2c, but given the quarter was already largely booked when CCL issued September predictions, investors are more attuned to outlook for next fiscal year. And it's not so good: EPS view of $2.20-$2.40 improves upon $1.88 this year -- which CEO \uc2 Micky Arison\uc2  calls most challenging in CCL's history -- but is short of consensus for $2.49. Shares fall 4% premarket to $37.54, pulling down peer Royal Caribbean (RCL) 2.1% along with it. (joan.solsman@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 9:27 EST - Initial analyst commentary appears positive to the prospects of ICE's planned $8B acquisition of \uc2 NYSE Euronext\uc2  (NYX). Antitrust--a bugaboo to past proposed deals in the sector--don't appear to be a worry here, some investment banks note. Meanwhile, \uc2 Stifel Nicolaus\uc2  points out that NYX's valuation is approximately the same as what ICE was willing to pay last year for a carved-out Liffe, a "premier franchise with a wide economic moat and limited viable competition." ICE is up 5.2% premarket while NYX surges 39%.(christopher.dieterich@dowjones.com; @chrisdieterich)\par \par \uc2 9:24 EST - Colombia's oil-driven economy had a good run in recent years -- it grew a strong 5.9% in 2011 year-on-year and expansion in 1H of 2012 was a respectable 4.8%. But 3Q GDP, due out at 11:00am ET, is likely to see the lowest quarterly growth rate in two years, about 3.8%, says a Dow Jones survey, due to a contraction in much-needed infrastructure spending. Adding to downside risks, Oct retail sales released last night declined 0.3%. Don't rule out a Friday \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate cut if today's GDP data falls below forecasts. (dan.molinski@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 9:17 EST - As the Brazilian real strengthens for the third consecutive day, investors are likely to test where the perceived floor is for the pair's new 2.05-2.10 band, according to \uc2 Brown Brothers Harriman\uc2 . Previously this year, \b \highlight22\uc2 Brazil\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to maintain a 2.00-2.10 band. "Action (or inaction) as we approach [the new band's floor] level will be very important, but our feeling is that Brazil still favors a weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  over a firmer one." USD/BRL recently at 2.0632, from 2.0720 late Wednesday, according to CQG. (georgia.wells@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 9:16 EST - Desjardins says the strong upside surprise to Canada's October retail sales is soothing because it shows there some level of support to domestic demand, which is under significant strains as a result of what looks like still-soft business-investment growth. However, it's worrying because households--contending with record-high debt--should be passing the baton to businesses, which have extremely high levels of liquid assets. (don.curren@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 9:12 EST - In William Blair's 8th annual online-merchandising study, it says Amazon (AMZN) now 150 times greater than the websites of \uc2 Wal-Mart\uc2  (WMT) and Target (TGT) in the 20 "general-merchandise categories" part of the study, versus 100 times a year ago as the brick-and-mortar firms continue to cut back offering in those segments. The review also finds WMT and TGT continue to lose the price battle, with AMZN's discounts to the others adding 1-2 percentage points on-year to 2% versus walmart.com and 9% compared with target.com. That as TGT's website for a 3rd-straight year raised prices on items available on AMZN. "We also believe it is now a first\u8208\'20\'10order consideration why the holiday-selling season has become so daunting for Target in recent years." (karen.talley@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 9:12 EST - \uc2 Nasdaq OMX Group\uc2  (NDAQ) shares climb 6.1% to $26.76 in premarket trading after \uc2 IntercontinentalExchange (ICE) \uc2 announces its acquisition of Big Board operator \uc2 NYSE Euronext\uc2  (NYX). NDAQ's effort to jointly acquire NYSE with ICE was thwarted by regulators last year. Shares of ICE's rival, \uc2 CME Group\uc2  (\uc2 CME\uc2 ), also up 1.7% premarket. (christopher.dieterich@dowjones.com; @chrisdieterich)\par \par \uc2 9:08 EST - Junk bond yields hit a record-low yesterday and could be on their way to break through the 6% barrier for the first time, according to the Barclays US High-Yield Index. Average yields have dropped to 6.06% from 6.42% at the start of the month. Total return in the index is 1.57% this month, lifting the 2012 return to 15.8% -- its best annual return since 2009. Meantime, high-grade bonds have lagged recently as Treasury yields climb. This month high-grade bonds have shed 0.43%, cutting the YTD total return to 9.42%. (patrick.mcgee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 9:04 EST - Not everyone is jumping on the bank-stock bandwagon. While \uc2 Meredith Whitney\uc2  and \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  have gone bullish on the sector and select big names, \uc2 JMP Securities\uc2  remains solidly pessimistic. Firm put a sell on big banks earlier this year and is keeping it there, with a note this morning pointing out that "fundamentals remain weak," bulge stocks are captive to fiscal cliff negotiations and still-lurking EU debt crisis. Note lowers forecasts for capital markets activity, with trading revenue down 19% from last quarter, and reduces 4Q estimates for \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  (C) and \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  (MS). "Our base case," firm says, "is that something goes wrong." C, MS both up fractionally premarket. (christian.berthelsen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 9:02 EST - Allscripts (MDRX) conducted a "thorough and rigorous process" to review strategic options, including a possible sale, before deciding the best move was to develop electronic-health-records firm's long-term potential under new management, Chairman \uc2 Dennis Chookaszian\uc2  says during today's conference call. MDRX abruptly switched CEOs Wednesday. The board "very seriously considered" its options, Chookaszian contends while thanking outgoing CEO \uc2 Glen Tullman\uc2  for how he grew MDRX during his 15-year tenure. But the chairman says Tullman and the board agreed it was time for a new leader. As the stock's buyout premium leaks out, MDRX is down 16% premarket at $8.95. (jon.kamp@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 8:58 EST - IntercontinentalExchange's (ICE) new exchange entity will match \uc2 NYSE Euronext\uc2 's (NYX) current dividend, according to a release. The new entitity would pay shareholders $300M in dividends payouts annually, the aggregate of NYSE's current dividends. (christopher.dieterich@dowjones.com; @chrisdieterich)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par To contact the Market Talk Editors, \par Bradley Davis, 212 416-2654, bradley.davis@dowjones.com \par Kevin Kingsbury, 212 416-2354, kevin.kingsbury@dowjones.com \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 John Shipman\f28 \uc2 \f2 , 212 416-2181,john.shipman@dowjones.com \par Patrick Sullivan, 212 416-2326,Patrick.sullivan@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 12-20-12 0932ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4035   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020121220e8ck0007v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
